<h1>Lingnan Daily — 61 Tales from South China</h1>
<h2>Introduction: How a Mountain Girl Found the World in a Bowl of Lychees</h2>

<p>**Lingnan Daily — 61 Tales from South China**</p>

<p>**Introduction: How a Mountain Girl Found the World in a Bowl of Lychees**</p>

<p>I was born in a remote mountain village in Jianghua County, southern Hunan — a place where the Nanling Mountains rise like a wall between the central plains and the south. On the other side of those mountains lies Lingnan: a world of tidal flats, ancestral temples, Cantonese opera, and the intoxicating sweetness of lychees ripening in June.</p>

<p>Growing up in the 1980s, I knew nothing of this. The mountains were a barrier, not a passage. I left my mountain village for the county town, working odd jobs while studying for the college entrance exam. After graduating, I taught high school in the county for six years. But the desire to see a bigger world pulled me toward the Pearl River Delta — then the very frontier of China's reform and opening-up.</p>

<p>By the 1990s, through sheer learning ability, I earned my place in the global supply chain management team of a multinational corporation — a team that, at the time, rarely included local Chinese talent. I stayed in this field for over two decades. For nearly a decade, I lived in the heart of Lingnan — Guangzhou, Shenzhen, Zhuhai, Dongguan, Foshan, Zhongshan, Huizhou. Their streets, factories, night markets, and ancestral halls became my daily life. I drank congee at four in the morning, napped to the drumbeats of dragon boats during the Dragon Boat Festival, watched countless sunsets along the Pearl River. Back then, I didn't know the term &quot;Lingnan culture.&quot; But I lived it.</p>

<p>From there I moved to the Yangtze River Delta, spending another decade in a different kind of water country. Two river deltas, two sets of dialects, two ways of life. This crossing taught me something profound — Lingnan is not one culture. It is a mosaic of five.</p>

<p>In the 2020s, I earned my DBA from emlyon business school in France and founded Beacon Consulting Co., Ltd. — my One Person Company. I now work with a team of AI agents from a laptop, navigating the currents of globalization. The girl who crossed the Nanling Mountains to find the world is now, through an OPC, paying homage to the culture south of those ridges.</p>

<p>This series is the result. Sixty-one tales — one for each day — shared in English across X and Facebook to tell the world about China's regional cultures. They span five worlds: folk tales, theater and dance, architecture, food, and landscapes. Some are legends a thousand years old. Some are festivals you can still attend this weekend. All of them are alive.</p>

<p>Why tell these stories to an international audience? Because Lingnan — this region south of the Five Ridges — is not a museum. It is a way of being in the world that has survived for millennia through trade, migration, adaptation, and sheer stubborn joy. It is the culture of people who built fortresses against pirates, who turned clay and gold thread into art, who believe that a bowl of noodles can carry the memory of a Ming Dynasty well.</p>

<p>If you are Chinese, you may recognize some of these stories. If you are not, you are about to discover one of the most vibrant, complex, and under-told regional cultures on the planet.</p>

<p>Either way, welcome to Lingnan.</p>

<p>— Connie Wu</p>

<p>Shanghai, June 2026</p>

<p>---</p>

<hr />

<h2>Part 1: Folk Tales</h2>

<h3>Story 1: Han Ma (Cold Mother)</h3>

<p>Imagine hiking a steep mountain trail in a snowstorm, body weak from hunger, clothes thin against the wind. That's exactly what a widow from Puning did in 1808. She climbed Wangtianling Mountain to bring winter clothes to her aunt who was gathering firewood. She never made it back.</p>

<p>When scholar Li Guangxian found her frozen body on his way to the imperial exam in Beijing, he faced an impossible choice. Bury her properly and miss the once-in-a-lifetime exam? Or leave her exposed to the elements? He could do neither. So he removed his blue cloth gown, covered her body, and made a promise: if he passed, he would return and build her a proper temple.</p>

<p>Here's where the story turns strange. The chief examiner noticed Li's essay had a stroke missing from the character &quot;horse&quot; — the kind of mistake that means instant failure. He reached to mark it wrong, but an ant filled the gap. He flicked it away. The ant crawled back. This happened several times, the ant stubbornly protecting Li's paper. Puzzled, the examiner summoned Li and learned of his kindness on the mountain path. Moved by the story — and believing Heaven itself had intervened — he passed Li on the spot.</p>

<p>Li kept his word. He built the Han Po Temple at the exact spot where he found her, inscribing the name himself on a golden plaque. Over time, locals renamed the woman &quot;Han Ma&quot; (Cold Mother) instead of &quot;Han Po&quot; (Cold Old Woman), softening the name with maternal warmth. The nameless path became &quot;Han Ma Path.&quot; For generations, travelers passing by would leave bundles of firewood at the temple, lighting small fires to keep her warm in spirit.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 2: Guifei Litchi (Concubine's Smile)</h3>

<p>What would you do for someone you love? In the 8th century, Emperor Xuanzong of Tang answered that question with one of history's most extravagant romantic gestures.</p>

<p>His beloved Concubine Yang — Yang Guifei, one of China's legendary Four Beauties — had a simple but urgent craving: fresh litchi. The problem? They were in Chang'an (modern Xi'an), roughly 2,000 km from the nearest litchi groves in Guangdong. And litchi is famously impossible to transport: its color changes in one day, its aroma shifts in two, its flavor fades by day three. By day four or five, it's completely worthless.</p>

<p>The emperor's solution was a logistical marvel that would impress modern supply chain experts. He built an express relay system: fast horses stationed at intervals, riders carrying bamboo tubes packed with freshly picked litchi from Gaozhou's Royal Orchard, thundering north along what became the Ancient Litchi Road. No rest, no delay — just the relentless sound of hooves racing against biological decay. Somehow, it worked. The fruit arrived in Chang'an in perfect condition.</p>

<p>This epic love story gave birth to the &quot;Feizixiao&quot; (Concubine's Smile) litchi variety — still grown in Gaozhou today. The fruit was later immortalized in poetry when Su Dongpo, the Song Dynasty's most famous foodie, misheard a Cantonese saying. Locals warned: &quot;One litchi equals three fires&quot; (meaning it's very heaty). Su heard &quot;eat 300 litchis a day&quot; and wrote the immortal line: &quot;Having 300 litchis a day, I'd never hesitate to be a Lingnanese.&quot;</p>

<p>Today, harvest festivals across Guangdong celebrate this heritage. But as locals say, the real treasure isn't the fruit itself — it's the story of what one emperor did for one smile.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 3: Xiangzi Bridge</h3>

<p>Just outside the east gate of Chaozhou's ancient city, spanning the broad Han River, sits one of China's four great ancient bridges. Bridge expert Mao Yisheng called it &quot;the world's earliest open-close bridge&quot; — a technical marvel with a removable central section made of boats. But locals tell a far better story than any engineering textbook.</p>

<p>Building it took over 300 years, from the first pier in the Song Dynasty to the final layout of 18 shuttle boats connecting 24 pier-islands. In those days, constructing a bridge across a raging river seemed impossible. So naturally, people said immortals built it.</p>

<p>The legend goes like this: Han Yu, the great Tang poet exiled to Chaozhou for speaking truth to power, wanted a way across the river. He asked for help. Monk Guangji took the west bank, using magic to turn stones into sheep and drive them to the construction site. The Eight Immortals took the east, turning rocks into pigs.</p>

<p>But supernatural construction has its perils. A widow's wailing broke Tieguai Li's spell — his stone pigs froze mid-march. Meanwhile, a greedy landlord claimed Guangji's sheep as his own. When the monk angrily agreed, the sheep became a mountain of black rocks, crushing the landlord's fields.</p>

<p>With rocks gone and the bridge unfinished, He Xiagu tossed her prized lotus into the river. Its petals became 18 shuttle boats forming a floating bridge. Guangji's walking stick became a vine binding them together.</p>

<p>And the two iron bullocks placed underwater to guard against floods? During a great Han River flood, one was swept away. Hence the ballad still sung today: &quot;Chaozhou's Xiangzi Bridge is fantastic — 18 boats on 24 piers, 24 towers in 24 styles, and two bullocks — but one slipped away.&quot;</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 4: Ying Lao Ye — Worshipping the Deities</h3>

<p>Walk into any village in eastern Guangdong and you'll find them: small temples built by local families, humble yet well-maintained, with incense always burning inside. The locals call the deities &quot;Lao Ye&quot; — an old, respectful term for gods. But here's the thing about Chaoshan people: they don't just pray to their gods. They carry them through the streets.</p>

<p>&quot;Ying Lao Ye&quot; is a tradition with roots stretching back 5,000 years. When Han Chinese migrated south from the Central Plains during the Qin and Han dynasties, they brought their gods with them. The indigenous Min-Yue people already had their own deities. Over centuries of coexistence, these two streams of belief merged into something uniquely Chaoshan.</p>

<p>The word &quot;ying&quot; tells the story. In the Chaozhou dialect — widely considered one of the most conservative living Chinese dialects, a living fossil of ancient speech — &quot;ying&quot; means &quot;to walk back and forth.&quot; In the Book of Poetry, it describes flies buzzing. So &quot;Ying Lao Ye&quot; literally means &quot;parading the gods around&quot; — carrying their statues through every lane so they can see their territory and bless the people.</p>

<p>On the big day, the community's strongest men — newlyweds get priority, symbolizing new life and luck — lift the deity's sedan chair onto their shoulders. Behind them comes a river of humanity: lion dancers, drummers, suona horn players, Chao Opera performers in full costume, villagers carrying incense and paper offerings. The most important god honored this way is the King of Three Mountains, whose temple in Jiexi County has stood for over 1,400 years.</p>

<p>The streets fill with smoke from incense. Drums thunder. The boundary between heaven and earth blurs. For one day, the gods walk among their people.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 5: Selling Yourself Festival</h3>

<p>If you visit Dongkeng Town in Dongguan on the second day of the second lunar month, you'll see something extraordinary. Thousands of people, young and old, armed with water guns, laughing as they drench complete strangers. It's a festival called &quot;Selling Yourself.&quot; Yes, that's the real name.</p>

<p>Four hundred years ago, this was pure peasant pragmatism. A wealthy landlord in Tangchun Village needed farmhands before the Qingming plowing season. He posted a notice on the local market day — the second day of the second lunar month. Soon, landless young men gathered, sitting on the village fence in bamboo hats and coarse cloth, signaling they were &quot;selling themselves&quot; — offering their labor to the highest bidder. The name stuck.</p>

<p>But legends grew around this humble beginning. People whispered that celestial immortals descended to watch the market, doing good deeds and blessing the righteous. Stories spread of miraculous encounters: a bent grinding arm straightened by a mysterious old man, a straight fishhook that caught endless fish. The festival acquired a second name: &quot;Meeting-an-Immortal Festival.&quot; Everyone walked the streets hoping to stumble upon celestial luck.</p>

<p>And here's the magical part nobody can explain: it always rains on February 2nd of the lunar calendar. Half sunny, half rainy, without fail for centuries. Water means money in Chinese culture. So decades ago, someone brought a water gun. Then everyone did. Now the main event is a city-wide water fight.</p>

<p>There's also lion dancing, folk performances, and streets lined with snacks. In 2007, it became a provincial intangible cultural heritage. Historians call it &quot;Lingnan's earliest labor market.&quot; From desperate farmhands selling their sweat to tourists drenching each other with water guns — the story of how a festival evolved is almost as wild as the festival itself.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 6: July Seventh Water</h3>

<p>On the seventh day of the seventh lunar month — China's Qixi Festival, often called Chinese Valentine's Day — something strange happens in Taishan, Guangdong. Before sunrise, entire families head to the nearest stream, river, or well. They carry buckets, jars, and bottles. They're collecting &quot;July Seventh Water.&quot;</p>

<p>The belief is centuries old, recorded in county annals from the Qing Dynasty and the Republic of China era. &quot;Store the river water in a urn on Qixi,&quot; reads the Cangwu County Annals, &quot;and it will never spoil. Call it 'Galaxy Water.'&quot; The Yangjiang Annals adds: &quot;At noon, store fresh well water to mix with medicine. It will not rot for years. Call it 'Immortal Water.'&quot;</p>

<p>What makes July 7th so special? Legend says seven fairies come down to earth that night, strip off their clothes, and swim naked in the rivers. Their fairy breath infuses the water with supernatural properties. It can cure heat stroke, colds, and skin rashes. Wash your hair with it — clearer mind. Wipe your eyes — better vision. Drink it — stronger stomach.</p>

<p>Old folks in Longwen Village still say: &quot;Wash away evil spirits by bathing in the river on July 7th.&quot; Parents send their children to swim, believing it washes away bad luck. Some even claim you can see the seven fairies bathing in the evening shadows.</p>

<p>Today, families store the water in sealed jars, sometimes with wax gourds inside, aging it like fine wine for three years before drinking — a cool, detoxifying tonic.</p>

<p>Science can't explain it. But Taishan people don't care. It's tradition. It's magic. And it works.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 7: Chaoshan Naore</h3>

<p>There's a word in the Chaozhou dialect that makes every Chaoshan person's eyes light up: &quot;Naore.&quot; Some say &quot;Laore.&quot; It literally means &quot;lively and bustling&quot; — but that undersells it completely. For Chaoshan people, Naore is bigger than Chinese New Year.</p>

<p>Between the first and second lunar months, every village across Chaozhou, Shantou, and Jieyang holds its own Naore on a different date. This means the festival rolls across the entire region for weeks, each village taking its turn. The tradition is over a thousand years old, born from ancient rituals of land worship that predate written history in the region.</p>

<p>Here's what happens when a village celebrates Naore: Every street is hung with colored silk and lanterns. In front of the ancestral hall, a temporary &quot;Shenqian&quot; — god's front — is built as an altar. Families bring red lacquer trays piled with offerings: whole roast pigs, chickens, steamed buns shaped like peaches, the Chaoshan &quot;red peach kuih&quot; — all arranged in orderly rows before the deities.</p>

<p>Then comes the main event. Strong young men — selected and trained in advance — lift the god's sedan chair onto their shoulders. Behind them, a magnificent parade forms: flag bearers, lion dance troupes, gong-and-drum teams, Chao Opera performers playing suona horns, and villagers of all ages carrying smaller banners. They wind through every alley and main road, blessing the entire village territory before returning to the ancestral hall.</p>

<p>And the food. Every family hires a professional chef. Tables groan with dishes. Relatives from Guangzhou, Shenzhen, and overseas flock home. &quot;Shi Laore&quot; — eating Naore — is sacred obligation. If you're Chaoshan, no excuse is good enough to miss it.</p>

<p>The incense smoke, the deafening drums, the specific taste of your village's cooking — Naore is the heartbeat of Chaoshan identity.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 8: Shilong Clogs</h3>

<p>The clogs of Shilong Town in Dongguan are more than footwear. They're canvases. Each pair is hand-painted with red lacquer and traditional motifs — pine trees and cranes for longevity, dragons and phoenixes for power, blooming flowers for prosperity. Walking in them on the old bluestone roads makes music: a crisp, satisfying clack that announces your arrival.</p>

<p>Why clogs, though? Think about Lingnan: hot, humid, with monsoon rains that turn streets into rivers. In the old days, people couldn't afford shoes and socks for everyday wear. But clogs — with their raised wooden soles — lifted feet above the puddles. More practical than straw sandals (which disintegrated), more breathable than leather shoes (which rotted). The perfect monsoon footwear.</p>

<p>The origin story goes back 2,600 years to one of China's most tragic tales. Duke Wen of Jin, a Spring and Autumn Period ruler, burned a mountain trying to force his loyal advisor Jie Zitui out of hiding. Jie refused to come out — he died embracing a tree. The grief-stricken Duke had the tree's roots carved into clogs, wore them constantly, and called the footwear &quot;under the foot&quot; — a term that became a Chinese honorific still used today when you address someone respectfully.</p>

<p>In Dongguan, clogs marked every life milestone. Brides walked out of their family homes in red clogs on wedding day. New homeowners wore them for good luck — the raised sole meant &quot;rising step by step.&quot; Students leaving for overseas study packed a pair as a talisman. One month after marriage, two pairs of baby-sized clogs would appear in the couple's room — a prayer for children.</p>

<p>But here's the hard truth that keeps culture lovers up at night: Liang Jingquan is now Dongguan's only remaining master of this craft. Nearly 30 years carving and painting. Nearly 20 steps to finish a single pair. And no apprentices in sight. To keep the tradition alive, he now visits schools and teaches at cultural events, hoping someone, anyone, will pick up the brush before it's too late.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 9: The Legend of the Wu Family</h3>

<p>&quot;The Wu family has a history stretching back thousands of years,&quot; writes Xiaoqian Wu, a student who traced her own ancestry for this story. She's describing something genuinely ancient — a surname lineage that runs parallel to Chinese civilization itself.</p>

<p>The Wu surname is one of China's oldest, dating to the Shang Dynasty over 3,000 years ago. But the original Wu weren't ordinary people — they were sorcerers and diviners, the spiritual counselors to kings and emperors. In the Shang Dynasty alone, the Wu family produced two prime ministers (father and son) who managed the entire kingdom. These were aristocrats who could communicate with heaven.</p>

<p>The family's original homeland was Pingyang in what is now Zou County, Shandong Province. From there, they spread across China like roots from a single tree trunk. Four main branches emerged: one in Shanxi (with Wu Gan as the founding ancestor), one in Fujian's Ninghua County (Wu Luojun), a second in Fujian's Yongding County (Wu Qi), and one in Guangdong's Xingning County (Wu Xi). Nearly every Wu descendant worldwide can trace their lineage to one of these four.</p>

<p>During the Southern Song Dynasty, roughly 1,000 years ago, the family's second great migration occurred. Seven brothers — the 17th generation — moved from Yongding, Fujian, to Guangdong's Shaoguan and fanned out across Zhaoqing, Zhongshan, Yangjiang, and Taishan. Today, you'll find Wu descendants across Guangdong and throughout the overseas Chinese diaspora.</p>

<p>Xiaoqian's family, now in Huaiji County, still makes the annual drive to neighboring Guangning County to worship their ancestors together. Though generations of migration have diluted their Hakka language, on worship day they greet each other in Hakka — one thread of language preserving the connection to a 3,000-year lineage.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 10: Nianli Festival — Nostalgia in Rituals</h3>

<p>In Maoming and Zhanjiang, western Guangdong, there's a saying you hear everywhere: &quot;Nianli is bigger than the New Year.&quot; And they mean it. For three full days — the &quot;Beginning,&quot; the &quot;Main Event,&quot; and the &quot;End&quot; — entire villages transform into something between a religious festival and a family reunion on steroids.</p>

<p>The name Nianli simply means &quot;annual tradition,&quot; but its roots run through one of southern China's most remarkable historical figures: Lady Xian. Born in the 6th century, she was a military leader and diplomat who unified the region, negotiated peace between ethnic groups, and commanded her own army at a time when women rarely held any public power. The imperial court granted her the title &quot;Lady of the Qiao State.&quot; During Nianli, her statue leads the parade, a silent reminder of the woman who shaped the region.</p>

<p>The entertainment is spectacular. &quot;Piaose&quot; — &quot;floating colors&quot; — features costumed children mounted on hidden iron poles, suspended above the crowd as gods and heroes. Zhenlong Town's Piaose is considered the finest example of this art in the region. &quot;Ti Daxi&quot; — Cantonese opera — runs for days, performed on special stages built for the occasion and lit by strings of lanterns.</p>

<p>There are also unique traditions: if a baby boy was born in the past year, his family lights a special lantern at the village temple that must never go out for the entire festival. The day before, they host a &quot;lighting feast&quot; for the whole community.</p>

<p>But the real heart of Nianli is reunion. Every Maoming person working in Guangzhou, Shenzhen, or overseas comes home. They eat, they pray, they watch the parade. The sound of drums echoes through streets that have held this tradition for over a thousand years. Nostalgia made visible.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Part 2: Theater & Dance</h2>

<h3>Story 11: Teochew Opera</h3>

<p>If you've never heard a Teochew Opera singer hold a note for what feels like an eternity, you haven't experienced southern China's most dramatic art form.</p>

<p>Teochew Opera — also called Chaodiao, Baizi, or simply &quot;the Chaozhou sound&quot; — has been telling love stories, war epics, and family dramas for over 400 years. Born when Song and Yuan dynasty musicals crashed into local Chaoshan folk traditions, it created something completely unique. By the Ming dynasty, it was already perfected.</p>

<p>But here's the real magic: the music itself. Teochew Opera uses no fewer than five distinct tune families to shape emotion. Soft three-six tunes for happiness and lightness. Vibrating five tunes for heartbreak and sorrow. Heavy three-six tunes for solemn ceremony. A quail tune for tragedy that cuts straight to the bone. A south branch tune that sounds like faded memory. And then the &quot;four stinky tunes&quot; — a rapid mashup of all four modes that locals cheekily named for its chaotic brilliance. Each melody shifts the mood of the entire hall in an instant.</p>

<p>The repertoire draws from two great sources. Some plays come directly from Song and Yuan dynasty scripts — ancient tales like The Story of Liu Xibi and The Tale of Pipa, unearthed as handwritten manuscripts that prove the opera's lineage. Others are adapted from local Chaoshan folklore, like Chen San and Wu Niang, a beloved love story about Chen Boqing and Huang Wuniang that has made generations of grandmothers cry.</p>

<p>Yet today, Teochew Opera faces an existential crisis. It's slow. It's in an ancient dialect. The stories are medieval. Against TikTok, Netflix, and K-pop, it struggles to hold young attention spans. The audience skews dangerously old.</p>

<p>But there's hope. Some troupes are keeping the classic tunes while rewriting the stories with modern themes and faster pacing. Overseas Chinese communities still treasure it as a lifeline to home — the sound of every Chaoshan grandparent's childhood. That emotional connection may be what saves it.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 12: Zijin Hua-Chao Opera</h3>

<p>The story of Hua-Chao Opera begins with a crisis that could have broken a community — but instead gave birth to an art form.</p>

<p>In the 26th year of Emperor Kangxi's reign, around 1687, a terrible epidemic swept through Yong'an County in Guangdong's Zijin mountains. No medicine could stop it. People were dying, and fear gripped every household. So the community turned to the only power they had left: the gods.</p>

<p>They hired local shamans — folk witches and wizards — to perform elaborate ceremonies. Singing, dancing, and ritual invocations were used to call upon divine spirits for protection and healing. What started as a desperate plea for survival slowly transformed into something beautiful. Over three centuries, those ritual dances evolved into a full theatrical tradition.</p>

<p>Today, Hua-Chao Opera has over 150 plays in its repertoire. Its subjects are drawn directly from everyday life: farmers selling their goods in the market, young women picking autumn flowers, legendary folk heroes from Hakka history. These are not tales of emperors and generals — they are stories of the people, told by the people.</p>

<p>The music is a glorious fusion of over 100 different ditties. Shenchao tunes, folk melodies, and Hakka mountain songs blend into something light, lyrical, and deeply emotional. The instruments are a miniature Chinese orchestra: gaohu, erhu, dulcimer, kun pipes, and more. The singing is simple and warm, often led by one voice with the whole community joining the chorus.</p>

<p>At its peak, ten thousand people would gather to watch a single performance — an entire mountainside of human voices and swaying lanterns. In 2006, China officially recognized Hua-Chao Opera as a National Intangible Cultural Heritage. The old shamans would be proud.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 13: Zhengzi Opera — &quot;Living Fossil&quot; of Southern Opera</h3>

<p>Zhengzi Opera is not just old. It's ancient — and it holds secrets from the very beginning of Chinese theater.</p>

<p>This &quot;living fossil&quot; traces its roots directly to the Southern Opera of the early Ming Dynasty. While other Chinese opera forms evolved and transformed, Zhengzi preserved its original structure, performing style, and musical system almost intact. A branch of southern opera drifted eastward and took root in the Hailufeng area of Guangdong, where it has survived for over 600 years.</p>

<p>What makes Zhengzi truly unique? It's performed in &quot;Zhongzhou Mandarin&quot; — the court language of medieval China — not in local Cantonese or Hakka dialect. That alone sets it apart from almost every other Lingnan theater tradition. Performed correctly, Zhengzi sounds like a window into Ming dynasty performance halls.</p>

<p>The repertoire is staggering: over 2,600 traditional plays, divided into two main categories. &quot;Wenxi&quot; or singing operas feature elegant vocal work blending Zhengyin singing with Kunqu opera tunes. The voice is led by the erhu, while flutes and suona horns add texture. &quot;Military plays&quot; are the opposite — packed with acrobatic combat, sword fights, and stylized battle choreography, with little to no singing. Just drums, gongs, and the raw energy of war.</p>

<p>And then there's the face paint — a code you can read at a glance. Red means loyalty and hot-blooded courage, like Guan Yu. Black signals seriousness, bravery, and strategic wisdom. White? Suspicion and cunning — the villain's mark. A character's entire moral essence is declared before they speak a single line.</p>

<p>Zhengzi has traveled far: Hong Kong, Macao, Taiwan, and across Southeast Asia. But its true home remains the temple stages of eastern Guangdong, where the old tunes still echo.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 14: Jieyang Iron-stick Puppet Show</h3>

<p>Welcome to the most delightful trick in Lingnan theater: the Jieyang Iron-stick Puppet Show — where tiny wooden actors perform full Chao Opera productions with more drama and agility than any human cast.</p>

<p>It began as shadow puppetry, brought south by migrants during the Southern Song Dynasty. For centuries, artists projected paper figures behind a white screen, telling classic stories in silhouette against candlelight. Audiences loved the art form but craved something more.</p>

<p>Then someone had a brilliant, almost rebellious idea: &quot;Why are we hiding the puppets behind a screen?&quot;</p>

<p>So they ripped down the backdrop. They replaced the flat paper figures with three-dimensional puppets — heads sculpted from fine clay with painted Chao Opera masks, bodies wrapped in miniature costumes, limbs connected by leather and wood. Each puppet stands just 24 to 45 centimeters tall. And three iron sticks extend from its back and hands, allowing the puppeteer to make it walk, fight, bow, and even spit fire.</p>

<p>The result was revolutionary. Called &quot;Round Body Paper Shadow&quot; at first, it soon became known as the Iron-stick Puppet Show — and was nicknamed &quot;mini Chao Opera&quot; because its repertoire and performance style mirrors the full human opera.</p>

<p>Here's the wildest part: a full show is performed by just five to seven people backstage. They sing, narrate, and play instruments simultaneously, covering every role from young maiden to warrior general. There's a famous Chaoshan saying: &quot;Beating drums by hands, beating gongs by feet, singing by mouth, hitting gong by head&quot; — describing a single performer doing everything at once.</p>

<p>In 2011, this astonishing art form was inscribed on China's National Intangible Cultural Heritage list. If you ever visit Jieyang, find a stage. The puppets are waiting.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 15: Longchuan Hand Puppet Show</h3>

<p>Every great puppet show in China has a hometown. Longchuan's story begins with two travelers and a bundle of string puppets.</p>

<p>In 1914, two men named Luo Qi and Luo San carried their puppets all the way from Quanzhou, Fujian Province, to Longchuan County in Guangdong. What they brought was a seed that grew into one of Guangdong's most cherished Hakka folk traditions.</p>

<p>The puppet show itself has ancient roots in China — historical records trace it back to the Han Dynasty. It flourished in the Tang, developed further in the Song, and spread across the country by the Ming and Qing dynasties. Along the way, distinct regional styles emerged: Jinjiang glove puppets, Zhangzhou glove puppets, Lingao puppets, and finally, Longchuan hand puppets.</p>

<p>What makes Longchuan special is its thoroughly Hakka character. The singing is mostly Hakka folk songs, the dialogue is in Hakka dialect, and the musical accompaniment draws from local folk ditties and tea-picking tunes. The puppets play all the classic Chinese opera roles: Sheng (the gentleman scholar), Dan (the elegant lady), Chou (the comic clown), and more. The performances range from touching love stories like Liang Shanbo and Zhu Yingtai to wild martial epics like Monkey King's Havoc in Heaven.</p>

<p>The popular troupes of the early days — &quot;Zhao Fengnian&quot; and &quot;He Fengnian&quot; in Hukou village — built a devoted following. But the road was never easy. In 1955, Longchuan's county puppet troupe was absorbed by Guangxi, and the tradition nearly disappeared. Yet dedicated artists kept performing in villages and schools, passing the craft from master to apprentice, generation after generation.</p>

<p>Today, Longchuan Hand Puppet Show survives as living proof that the simplest stage — just two hands and a wooden doll — can hold the biggest stories.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 16: Chaoshan Village Opera</h3>

<p>There is a passage from the Annals of Three Kingdoms that explains Chaoshan Village Opera better than any travelogue: &quot;The southern and northern suburbs of the city were places for worship. While the god of heaven was sacrificed with spirit and meat, the god of land was worshiped with opera.&quot;</p>

<p>Opera as worship. Drama as prayer. This is the philosophy that has sustained Chaoshan Village Opera for over a thousand years.</p>

<p>The tradition flourished during the Tang and Song dynasties, when almost every Chaoshan village had a &quot;Temple of the Land God,&quot; a &quot;Tugu Ancestral Hall,&quot; or an ancestral temple. Around each temple, residents formed social organizations that eventually grew into full communities. And every year, those communities staged an &quot;Opera of the Year&quot; for their gods — a tradition that continues unabated to this day.</p>

<p>These performances fall into several categories. Temple operas honor the gods on their birthdays. Ancestral temple operas pay respects to family lineages. Festival operas mark the seasons and harvests. But the grandest of all is the opera for Mazu, the Sea Goddess, on March 23rd of the lunar calendar.</p>

<p>Legend says Mazu saved countless fishermen from drowning and was honored by emperors with plaques and titles. Coastal communities prayed to her for safe voyages and abundant catches. On her birthday, the whole village gathers. On stage, glamorously costumed women sing the Chaoshan dialect with heartbreaking feeling. Elders in the audience frown, clap, weep — completely absorbed in every gesture of the performers.</p>

<p>And it's not just traditional opera anymore. Modern troupes also screen auspicious movies — Jackie Chan comedies, happy endings — because if the gods enjoy a good film too, why not? Puppet shows and shadow plays run alongside human performances. The village opera adapts.</p>

<p>Critics dismiss it as superstition and waste. Locals call it community — celebration of harvest, comfort for the weary, and the cultural lifeblood of rural China.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 17: Chaozhou Folk Songs</h3>

<p>In ancient China, private schools were built for one purpose: preparing boys for the imperial examinations. Girls stayed home. But in Chaoshan, the people found a brilliant workaround.</p>

<p>They created songbooks — and taught an entire generation of women through music.</p>

<p>Chaozhou Folk Songs — also known simply as &quot;Song&quot; or &quot;Laughing Song&quot; — are lyrics written in the Chaoshan dialect, seven characters per line, rhyming with the unique cadence of local street-corner speech. They evolved from Tang-dynasty Chaozhou storytelling and reached their peak during the Qing Dynasty's Qianlong and Jiaqing reigns, roughly the 18th century.</p>

<p>The subjects covered everything imaginable. Historical novels like The Romance of the Sui and Tang Dynasties were adapted into singable form. Famous court cases — Seven Corpses and Eight Lives — became gripping ballads. Romantic tales like Romance of the Western Chamber brought love stories to women who could not read novels. And local folklore, like the story of Su Liuniang, preserved regional identity in verse.</p>

<p>But the most remarkable thing about Chaozhou Folk Songs is not their content — it's who sang them. Women. Almost exclusively. Mothers sang to daughters. Literate girls taught their illiterate friends. By the Republic of China period, when new schools finally began admitting female students, educated girls voluntarily became teachers for their peers. Those who still couldn't attend school learned about history, culture, and morality through the songs.</p>

<p>Historians have called it &quot;the unique female culture in the history of Chaoshan&quot; — a grassroots education movement powered entirely by verses.</p>

<p>Then came the Cultural Revolution. Folk songs were labeled one of the &quot;Four Olds&quot; and banned. Books were burned by the thousands. Unlike popular novels, these songs were a niche art — once destroyed, they could never be fully recovered. The tradition nearly vanished.</p>

<p>Today, the surviving copies are preserved in museums and rare book libraries. In 2008, Chaozhou Folk Songs were inscribed on China's National Intangible Cultural Heritage list. The songs are silent now — but their echoes shaped generations.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 18: The Song of Huidong's Fishermen</h3>

<p>Huidong County sits on the coast of Guangdong, facing Daya Bay and the South China Sea. For centuries, the people here have been known as &quot;those who live in the back of the boat&quot; — entire communities that made their homes on the water, rarely setting foot on solid ground.</p>

<p>From this life on the waves, a musical tradition was born.</p>

<p>The Song of Huidong's Fishermen is one of Guangdong's most treasured folk arts. Unlike the polished court orchestras of northern China, these songs are raw, immediate, and deeply emotional. They describe the fisherman's life in simple, unadorned words: the wind filling the sails, the strain of pulling nets, the ache of waiting for a loved one to return from sea.</p>

<p>The history runs deep. According to Huidong County Annals, the ancestors of the local fishermen moved from Fujian and Chaozhou during the Song Dynasty, bringing their songs with them. Over centuries, the melodies mixed with Huidong dialect and local rhythms until a distinct tradition emerged — one as salty and untamed as the ocean itself.</p>

<p>The customs surrounding these songs are dynamic and ceremonial. This is not a static tradition like eating zongzi on Dragon Boat Festival. It's a living, moving culture — processions to commemorate Mazu, the Carp Lantern Dance of Pinghai, group chants that accompany ritual activities. Movement, collectivity, and participation are its core.</p>

<p>And the songs themselves carry not just beauty but pain. Historical records show that Huidong's fishermen suffered extreme hardship — oppression, poverty, and the constant danger of storms at sea. Their songs held that suffering. But they also held hope: hope that the next tide would bring a better catch, a safe return, a kinder world.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 19: Sihui Folk Songs</h3>

<p>In the city of Sihui, Zhaoqing area, there lives a singing tradition far older than most of the world's nations — and it's still being passed from grandmother to grandchild today.</p>

<p>Sihui Folk Songs are a branch of Hakka mountain songs. But here's the twist: over many centuries, the local Sihui dialect reshaped the pronunciation and intonation so thoroughly that these songs became something entirely their own. They sound distinctly different from the Hakka folk songs of Meizhou, Huizhou, Jiangxi, and Guangxi — a unique branch on the great tree of Chinese folk music.</p>

<p>The standard form is deceptively simple: four lines, seven characters each, every line ending with a perfect rhyme. Simple enough for anyone to learn. Complex enough to convey the full range of human emotion. The lyrics are born from the heart — singers are moved by the scenery around them and express their feelings in direct, vivid language. The melodies are catchy, loud, and clear.</p>

<p>Here's the remarkable evidence of their age: during the Chunxi period of the late Song Dynasty, between 1174 and 1189, a Sihui magistrate named Su Jianbanghe was building the county government office when workers unearthed an ancient stone monument. Carved into it was a poem that read: &quot;The singing style of Longling has the dragon's spirit; if a phoenix heard it, it will also sing.&quot;</p>

<p>That poem tells us Sihui folk songs were already celebrated before the year 1189. That's over 800 years of continuous oral tradition.</p>

<p>The songs cover a wide range of themes — love, labor, nature, celebration. They were never written down by the original singers. Instead, they were taught from heart to heart, mouth to ear, generation after generation. In 2015, the Guangdong government officially recognized Sihui Folk Songs as provincial intangible cultural heritage. A formal honor for eight centuries of spontaneous melody.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 20: Salty Water Song</h3>

<p>Close your eyes and imagine this scene: you are on a small boat drifting through the Pearl River Delta at sunset. The only sounds are water lapping against the wooden hull and a voice — clear, untrained, unaccompanied — singing across the rippling surface of the river. No instruments. No stage. Just a person on a boat, singing about the sea.</p>

<p>That is the Salty Water Song.</p>

<p>Also known as &quot;Cantonese Fishing Song&quot; or &quot;Zhongshan Folk Song,&quot; this tradition belongs to the Tanka people — China's &quot;boat people&quot; who lived their entire lives on the water. They were born on boats, married on boats, raised children on boats, and were buried from boats. Land was a foreign concept. The sea was home.</p>

<p>And the sea, which they called &quot;salty water,&quot; gave their music its name and its soul.</p>

<p>Salty Water Songs come in many forms for every occasion. Gaotang songs are for celebration — weddings, baby's first month, festival days — with light, cheerful melodies that lift the festive atmosphere. When two Tanka boats met for a wedding, the bride and groom would sing to each other. Whichever sang better won the right to take the spouse home. Daban songs, also called &quot;Gumei songs,&quot; are work chants sung during the fishermen's rest. Each line ends with the local rhyme &quot;Gu Ya Mei&quot; — a linguistic fingerprint of Tanka culture.</p>

<p>The Ming dynasty writer Qu Dajun recorded this tradition in his book The New Canton Dialect, proving Salty Water Songs were already deeply popular in the late Ming and early Qing dynasties — over 300 years of continuous tradition.</p>

<p>Together with Huidong fishing songs and Hakka hill songs, Salty Water Songs form the three great pillars of Cantonese folk music. They are a living archive of a people who called the ocean their home, and whose voice still carries across the waves.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 21: Ruhu Fishermen's Songs</h3>

<p>Picture a fisherman's life in Qing Dynasty China: born on a boat, living on a boat, dying on a boat. Forbidden from education, barred from imperial exams, denied the cultural life of those who walked on solid ground. Yet from this harsh existence came something beautiful — the Ruhu Fishermen's Songs.</p>

<p>These are not your typical coastal fishing ballads. Ruhu is a freshwater town on the upper Dongjiang River in Huizhou, where the water moves slowly and so does life. Legend says seven fairies once visited the area and were so enchanted they forgot to return to heaven — and the lake became known as Fairy Lake, later Ruhu Lake. The songs the local fishermen created are gentle, soothing, sung in the mild Huizhou dialect rather than the powerful, booming tones of their seafaring counterparts.</p>

<p>With no instruments — everything was improvised on tiny boats — the songs covered everything: work, love, weddings, funerals, even farming and children's rhymes. A daughter had to sing a &quot;crying song&quot; at her wedding or be considered unfilial. When a loved one passed, the family sang them to the afterlife.</p>

<p>Today, the fishermen have moved ashore. They now walk on solid ground, work the fields, and live in houses that don't rock with the current. But they still sing. Every note of the Ruhu fishing songs carries the memory of a floating village, water lapping at wooden hulls, a gentle breeze carrying voices across the river. It's the sound of a world that almost disappeared — and the echoes that remain.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 22: Guangdong Han Music</h3>

<p>If you listen closely to Guangdong Han Music, you can hear footsteps across a thousand years — the sound of an entire people migrating, carrying their civilization in their instruments.</p>

<p>When the Hakka people fled south from the Central Plains — escaping wars and upheavals that began as early as the Qin Dynasty — they brought with them something precious: the musical scores of ancient court music. Zhongzhou Ancient Melody. Hangao Ancient Music Score. These were no ordinary tunes. They were the echoes of imperial courts, the refined sounds of a sophisticated culture transplanted into the rugged mountains of eastern Guangdong.</p>

<p>In Meizhou's Dabu County — officially named &quot;Hometown of Guangdong Traditional Chinese Music&quot; in 2004 — this music took on new life. The ancient Han melodies fused with local folk music, temple music, and even absorbed elements of Chaozhou music. The result was a genre with five distinct forms: String Music (the most intimate), Music for Literati Activities (intellectual and refined), Festival Music (booming gongs and drums for celebrations), Ceremonial Music (for formal occasions), and Temple Music (solemn and spiritual).</p>

<p>But here's the sobering truth: Guangdong Han Music is endangered. Modern entertainment has pulled young audiences elsewhere. The older masters are passing away, taking their unique techniques with them. Temple Music players are especially rare — the solemn style doesn't match today's entertainment tastes. Most music scores remain scattered in private collections, not properly archived.</p>

<p>Yet the melody endures. In the hills of Meizhou, the strings still sing. It's a living museum — one that needs both preservation and a new audience to survive.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 23: Nuo Dance</h3>

<p>Imagine this: It's the 13th night of the first lunar month. The streets of Zhanjiang blaze with lanterns and firecrackers. Teams of 300-500 performers — their faces hidden behind painted wooden masks in five colors — move through the crowd to the thunder of gongs and drums. The air thickens with smoke and incense. This is Nuo Dance — one of the oldest, most primal folk dances in all of Lingnan.</p>

<p>Nuo Dance is called the &quot;living fossil&quot; of Chinese dance — and for good reason. It dates back thousands of years to ancient rituals aimed at one thing: driving away evil. The dancers, wearing masks painted in green, red, white, black, and yellow (representing the five thunder gods), perform a precise sequence of rituals: petitions to the gods, oaths of duty, the hunt for evil, the confinement of misfortune, and finally, celebration.</p>

<p>The most dramatic moment? When the Boat God appears. After the thunder gods have driven the evil spirits out of every household in the village — each home sets up an altar table to welcome them — the Boat God gathers the bad luck onto a paper boat. The boat is lit on fire and set adrift on the river, carrying misfortune away. Then the Earth God steps in, scattering treasures to children and grand-children for blessings.</p>

<p>Some of the Nuo masks still in existence are 600 years old, carved from wood and painted by hand. The dance preserves an ancient system of &quot;beating drums to expel epidemics&quot; — a ritual music system that predates written history.</p>

<p>In 2008, Nuo Dance was recognized as a national intangible cultural heritage. It's raw, wild, mysterious — and completely unforgettable.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 24: Spring Cattle Dance</h3>

<p>In Zijin County, Heyuan, when spring arrives, the villagers don't just celebrate — they dance. And not just any dance. Two people climb inside a giant fake cow made of bamboo strips, paper, and cloth. A shepherd boy leads the &quot;cattle&quot; onto the stage, and the hilarity begins.</p>

<p>The Spring Cattle Dance is exactly what it sounds like — a joyful, mischievous celebration of the ox, the farmer's most precious partner. The performers inside the costumed cow imitate every mannerism: licking, scratching, rolling, shaking. The shepherd boy rides the cow, teases it, feeds it grass, brushes its back. It's part dance, part comedy, and completely charming.</p>

<p>Historically, this was no mere folk pastime. In old China, county officials would lead a grand ceremony at the Wugu Temple to pray for good harvest. They'd shoulder sacrifices, lead a real ox to the fields, and the county magistrate himself would plow the first furrow — called &quot;demonstrating cultivation.&quot; When the harvest was abundant, the villagers would dance with the ox in pure gratitude.</p>

<p>Today, the dance has modernized. Men in head towels carry hoes onto the stage; women in coolie hats carry flower baskets. They circle, sing antiphonal duets about the 24 solar terms and farming activities, accompanied by suona and flute. The signature song — &quot;Busy Spring Farming with Muddy Feet&quot; — captures the rustic joy of working the land.</p>

<p>The Spring Cattle Dance made it all the way to Hong Kong in 1997, performing in Tuen Mun, Tai Po, and Yuen Long. And in 2007, it was listed as a Heyuan City intangible cultural heritage. Not bad for a dance where two people dress as a cow and make the whole village laugh.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 25: Beihua Dance (Cup Dance)</h3>

<p>What do you do with a teacup? Drink tea, obviously. But in Xingning, Guangdong, they do something entirely different with it — they dance with it.</p>

<p>The Beihua Dance — also called the Cup Flower Dance — is one of the most unique folk dances you'll ever see. Dozens of Hakka girls in traditional costumes step onto the stage, each holding two white porcelain cups called &quot;Bogong Cups.&quot; As the Hakka mountain song begins, they start to move — shaking the cups, tapping them together, spinning them in their palms. The sharp, crisp sound of porcelain against porcelain becomes part of the music itself.</p>

<p>But here's the twist: this dance started as Taoist ritual. Originally, performers used bamboo clappers from the &quot;five-sentence board&quot; storytelling tradition. Then someone had a brilliant idea — replace the clappers with local Xingning-made white porcelain cups. The sound was cleaner, brighter, more musical. And the Beihua Dance was born.</p>

<p>The technique is surprisingly complex. Dancers use &quot;shake, shake, beat, grind, turn&quot; skills — moving through cloud steps, cross steps, kneeling steps, and bending poses. The cups are thrown, caught, rolled across the hands. All while the dancers sing Hakka folk songs about love and daily life.</p>

<p>In 2007, it was recognized as a provincial intangible cultural heritage. In 2008, Xingning was named &quot;Hometown of the Chinese Folk Art Cup Flower Dance.&quot; The dance keeps innovating — changing themes from young love to community celebration, making the cups lighter and more skillful.</p>

<p>Close your eyes and picture it: moonlight, the sound of porcelain cups ringing in rhythm, girls in embroidered costumes spinning under lanterns. That's the Beihua Dance.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 26: Crane Dance</h3>

<p>Here's a wordplay that became a tradition: in the local dialect of Shen Mingting village (Zhongshan City), the word for &quot;crane&quot; (鹤, hè) sounds exactly like the word for &quot;learning&quot; (学, xué). So when the ancestors saw the elegant white cranes dancing by the rivers, they had an idea — what if we made a dance that celebrates both the bird and the pursuit of knowledge?</p>

<p>The Crane Dance was born. A giant white crane, woven from bamboo and covered in white cloth, with painted eyes and a red flower on its head. The bamboo frame is taken to the temple to be blessed. Then a dancer climbs inside and brings the crane to life.</p>

<p>The original dance was simple — just one crane following a &quot;crane song&quot; sung in the local dialect. But over the centuries it grew. Now there are 14 movements: walking in a circle, soaring with wings, playing with clams. The parade is the grandest event in the village. A performer dressed as the Deity Zi Wei leads the way (to drive away demons). Someone carries a wooden box of books (to honor learning). Civil and military figures walk on either side. And of course, there's a full band — gongs, drums, suona, and traditional Cantonese music.</p>

<p>Every ten years, during the Ming and Qing dynasties, the &quot;Out of the Crane&quot; convention was held — the biggest show in town.</p>

<p>But here's the heartbreaking part: the Crane Dance is on the verge of extinction. Young people in Shaxi prefer hip-hop, K-pop, Latin dance. The old masters are passing away. Fewer than a handful of performers remain, and they're all elderly. Without young hands to carry the bamboo crane, this 500-year-old dance may simply — vanish.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 27: Tangxia Kylin Dance</h3>

<p>What's the difference between a lion dance and a kylin dance? The personality. A kylin — a mythical chimeric creature in Chinese mythology — isn't just fierce. It's playful. Curious. Even a little cheeky.</p>

<p>In Tangxia Town, Dongguan, the Kylin Dance has been a tradition for over 300 years. According to the Dongguan County Annals from 1916, the dance teams would gather from New Year's Day to month-end, pasting paper kylin heads painted in five colors, quilting fabric bodies, and sending two dancers inside each beast. After the dance? Full-blown martial arts demonstrations — Mo's Boxing, Southern Style Boxing, Dragon Style Boxing, followed by 18 different weapons.</p>

<p>The dance itself is structured in two routines. The &quot;upper routine&quot; shows the kylin at play — licking its feet, shaking its tail, scratching itself. The &quot;lower routine&quot; tells a story: the kylin searches for the greens (xunqing), gets scared by them (jingqing), smells them (wenqing), tests them (shiqing), plucks them (caiqing), chews them (jiaoqing), and swallows them (tunqing). Every movement improvised, full of humor and character.</p>

<p>The real hero of this story? Huang Hanguang, the inheritor of the tradition. He realized the traditional single-kylin dance was too slow and monotonous for young audiences. So he redesigned it: multiple kylins (up to 9 at once!), faster tempo, more action, creating visual conflicts and dramatic highlights. He even developed a notation system to write down the drum scores that were previously passed down only by word of mouth.</p>

<p>Thanks to him, the Tangxia Kylin Dance was recognized as a provincial intangible cultural heritage in 2013. The beast lives on — now with a younger, faster heartbeat.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 28: Fire Dog Dance, Huizhou</h3>

<p>Of all the Lingnan folk dances, none is more unique — or more deeply meaningful — than the Fire Dog Dance of the Lantian Yao people.</p>

<p>The legend goes like this: an army of invaders was crushing the emperor's forces. No human could stop them. But a dog — magnificent, with flowing fur and a body like a tiger — came forward. The dog breathed fire and defeated the entire enemy army. The emperor promised his daughter's hand to whoever defeated the enemy, but he didn't want to marry her to a dog. His youngest daughter insisted — she was in awe of the divine hound. On the 15th night of the 8th lunar month, they married. Their four children were raised on dog's milk. That dog is the ancestor of the Lantian Yao.</p>

<p>So every Mid-Autumn Festival, the Yao perform the Fire Dog Dance. But not just anyone dances. This is a girls' coming-of-age ceremony. From age 16 to 18, every Yao girl must participate 2 to 3 times. Their mothers dress them in bamboo hats stuck with incense sticks, wrap their hands, waists, and feet in ginger leaves. They bow at the ancestral hall, visit the vegetable fields, pay respects to the Kitchen God at every house. Then they gather by the bonfire — dancing, singing Fire Dog Songs, holding torches. Finally, they throw the incense and ginger leaves into the river and sing love songs across the water to the Yao boys on the opposite bank.</p>

<p>But this beautiful tradition is fading. Modern Yao girls know the ceremony but not its meaning. Young people are too shy for public love songs. The old singers are passing away. The Fire Dog Dance needs listeners — or the fire may go out forever.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 29: Foshan Shadowless Kick</h3>

<p>When most people hear &quot;Foshan Shadowless Kick,&quot; they picture Wong Fei-hung — the legendary martial artist and folk hero — delivering an invisible blur of kicks that takes down a dozen enemies. Movie magic? Sure. But the real story is even more impressive.</p>

<p>In southern Chinese martial arts, most techniques focus on the upper body — waist, arms, hands. The legs are often neglected. Huang Feihong changed that. After years of traveling and exchanging martial knowledge between northern and southern schools, he fused northern footwork with southern boxing to create the Shadowless Kick.</p>

<p>The name says it all: the kick is so fast, the opponent can't see it coming. But the foundation isn't speed — it's stability. You can't kick like lightning if you're wobbling. The entire technique rests on one thing: horse stance.</p>

<p>And not just any horse stance. Yu Zhiwei, the third-generation successor of the technique, spent 12 YEARS practicing horse stance before his master deemed him &quot;stable enough&quot; to learn the kick. Twelve years of standing, breathing, building strength from the ground up. His master's words still echo: &quot;Shadowless kick is sharp — yet useless without solid basic skills.&quot;</p>

<p>The actual technique is a feint. First, the hands attack — fast, flashy, drawing the enemy's attention upward. Then, when the opponent is focused on defending their upper body, the legs strike below. The enemy falls before they even know what hit them.</p>

<p>Shadowless Kick isn't just a martial arts technique. It's a life lesson — that real mastery takes unimaginable patience, that the most powerful moves come from the most stable foundations. &quot;Constant grinding turns an iron rod into a needle,&quot; as the old saying goes.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Part 3: Architecture & Heritage</h2>

<h3>Story 30: Hidden Fortresses / 隐秘的炮台</h3>

<p>Hidden in plain sight in today's Shantou, Qilu Stone Fortress tells an incredible story of resilience — both of the people who built it and the structure that still stands defiantly a century and a half later.</p>

<p>In the mid-19th century, after the Opium Wars, the Qing government was forced to open Shantou as a trading port under the unequal Treaty of Tientsin. Foreign merchant ships flooded in, and coastal defenses were dangerously weak. General Fang Yao petitioned the court to build a fortress that could truly protect the region. The result? A circular castle covering 19,607 square meters, surrounded by a 23-meter-wide, 3-meter-deep moat.</p>

<p>But here's what makes it extraordinary: the walls weren't built with modern cement. They used a mixture of overcooked shell ash, sand, glutinous rice porridge, brown sugar, and granite stones — a formula that proved so effective that more than a century later, the entire building remains crack-free, a living testament to ancient ingenuity.</p>

<p>The fortress has 36 cannon positions (18 on each level), a 300-meter-long underground tunnel system, and 72 cleverly designed ventilation towers arranged in triangular groups for communication and airflow. A 27-step wave-shaped stone staircase allowed soldiers to haul cannons up to the platform quickly during battle, while a freshwater well in the northeast corner sustained the garrison through long sieges.</p>

<p>Built between 1874-1879 at a cost of 80,000 silver dollars, the fort once housed the massive 5,000-hu cannon with a range of 7.5 kilometers. The crescent-shaped parade ground still echoes with the imagined footsteps of soldiers who drilled and stood ready. Today it's a peaceful park where locals stroll — but the quiet exterior still whispers of wars fought, treaties broken, and a nation's determination to protect its shores.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 31: Bijiang Golden Tower, Shunde / 碧江金楼</h3>

<p>Shunde's Bijiang Golden Tower isn't just a building — it's a love story written in gold leaf, a piece of imperial China tucked away in a Lingnan water village.</p>

<p>Built during the Jiaqing and Daoguang reigns of the Qing Dynasty, this architectural complex started humbly as the Su family's private library. But when Su Boyu prepared to marry Dai Peiqiong — the goddaughter of Empress Dowager Cixi herself — the family made a bold decision. They covered the entire building in gold leaf, transforming a scholar's retreat into the most exquisite bridal chamber in all of Lingnan.</p>

<p>The complex spreads across multiple structures: mud brick houses, oyster-shell walls (a classic Lingnan feature that keeps interiors cool in summer and warm in winter), ancestral halls, study rooms, and gardens. The golden tower itself is a woodcarving masterpiece — every surface hand-carved with motifs that blend traditional Chinese aesthetics with subtle foreign influences, a reflection of Lingnan's unique role as a cultural crossroads.</p>

<p>Inside, the famous &quot;eight treasure bed&quot; remains as it was in the Qing dynasty. An official's sedan chair sits in the front corridor, preserved for well over a century. But the absolute highlight is the 23.8-meter-long, 1.5-meter-high bronze mural — the &quot;Bijiang version of Along the River During the Qingming Festival&quot; — created by Su He, a son of the Su family during the Daoguang period. Every detail of historical Bijiang life is captured in stunning, permanent relief.</p>

<p>What makes this place truly special is seeing how a Lingnan scholarly family lived across generations. From farming to Confucian scholarship to merchant wealth, the Su family's evolution mirrors the transformation of the entire Pearl River Delta. Walking through these halls as the evening sun catches the gold, you feel the weight of a civilization that truly mastered the art of living beautifully.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 32: Zhaoqing Song Dynasty City Wall / 肇庆宋城墙</h3>

<p>Zhaoqing's ancient city wall is one of the best-preserved Song Dynasty brick fortifications in all of China — and its story is both dramatic and surprisingly practical, involving not just soldiers and battles but also floods and survival.</p>

<p>Before the Song Dynasty, Zhaoqing had no walls at all. That changed catastrophically in 1052 when Nong Zhigao, a tribal leader from Guangxi, swept through the region conquering city after city. The local governor, Ding Baochen, had no defenses whatsoever and fled in panic. The court sent General Di Qing to crush the rebellion, and the lesson was clear: Zhaoqing needed real protection.</p>

<p>Mayor Jiang Jianzhi built the first earth ramparts in 1053, choosing the location carefully — positioned between General Mountain and the Xijiang River, following the principle of &quot;city by mountain and water.&quot; The wall proved so well-designed that subsequent dynasties kept adding to it. Today, the wall contains distinct bricks from Song, Yuan, Ming, Qing, and even the Republic of China period — with nine different brick sizes from the Song Dynasty alone. It's called the &quot;Brick Museum&quot; for good reason.</p>

<p>The four original gates — Songchong (east), Zhennan (west), Duanxi (south), and Chaotian (north) — each had imposing gate towers, plus barbican outer defenses and corner turrets. But the wall's most remarkable feature is what happened when the river rose.</p>

<p>Over 777 years, the Xijiang River recorded 104 major floods. In 1915, the river surged catastrophically, turning the outside world into an ocean. But behind the wall, the city stayed completely dry. Ming Dynasty engineers had cleverly linked the city wall to the river embankment, creating a dual-purpose defense against both invaders and nature's fury. That's Chinese engineering at its most brilliant — an ancient wall that proved more useful against water than against weapons.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 33: Inlay Porcelain / 嵌瓷</h3>

<p>Broken porcelain shards don't look like art — until Chaozhou's craftsmen work their magic, transforming what others see as garbage into rooftop masterpieces that have dazzled under the Lingnan sun for 400 years.</p>

<p>Inlay Porcelain, known locally as &quot;Tie Rao&quot; or &quot;Kou Rao,&quot; is a unique architectural decoration technique native to the Chaoshan region. It forms a trinity with Chaozhou woodcarving and stone carving as the three great folk architectural arts of the area. During the Ming Dynasty under Emperor Shenzong, local artisans had a stroke of genius: why not take broken ceramic pieces — normally discarded — and transform them into stunning rooftop decorations?</p>

<p>The process is painstaking. First, the master designs the entire composition — shape, size, color palette. A body is sculpted from lime and paper pulp to form the base. Each porcelain shard is then carefully clipped with shears to exactly the right size and shape. Finally they're inlaid one by one, coated with paper-lime paste, until a magnificent dragon, phoenix, or deity emerges in full color.</p>

<p>The subjects draw from folklore, history, opera, and mythology. Auspicious beasts like dragons and phoenixes symbolize prosperity. Historical figures embody moral virtues. Flowers and birds represent peace and harmony. These dazzling creations adorn ancestral halls, temples, and traditional houses — turning rooftops into storytellers.</p>

<p>Why did this art flourish specifically in Chaozhou? Three reasons. Porcelain is naturally colorfast and weather-resistant — crucial for withstanding Lingnan's harsh tropical sun and monsoon rains. Chaozhou was a historic porcelain capital, producing abundant broken shards as raw material. And the local culture of building grand ancestral halls created endless demand for decorative craftsmanship.</p>

<p>&quot;Break and then rise anew&quot; is the philosophy. What was once refuse becomes treasure — a beautiful metaphor for resilience that resonates far beyond architecture, deep into the spirit of the people who create it.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 34: Hakka Dragon Houses (Weilongwu) / 围龙屋</h3>

<p>If architecture reveals how a people see the world, then Hakka Dragon Houses tell you everything about the Hakka spirit.</p>

<p>Beginning in the Tang and Song dynasties, the Hakka people — &quot;guest families&quot; who migrated south from central China — built these extraordinary circular fortresses in the mountains of Meizhou and beyond. Known as Weilongwu (Dragon Houses), they're the physical embodiment of Hakka values: unity, hierarchy, self-sufficiency, and defense.</p>

<p>From the outside, a Dragon House looks like a closed fortress — high walls, few openings, easy to defend against bandits and attackers. Inside, it's a complete world radiating outward from the central ancestral hall. The circular layout wraps around and around in concentric rings, with bedrooms, kitchens, wells, chicken coops, pigsties, and warehouses for everything needed to live.</p>

<p>The architecture follows strict symmetry — reflecting the Confucian order of respect: primary and secondary, elder and younger, each family in its proper place. The ancestral hall sits at the center, as sacred to the Hakka as a church to its congregation.</p>

<p>Living in a Dragon House meant living as one big family. When a neighbor fell on hard times, everyone pitched in. Strangers passing through were greeted with warm Hakka hospitality: &quot;Come in and have a cup of tea.&quot; These round fortresses literally shaped the Hakka character — raising generations of people who value community, hard work, and mutual support.</p>

<p>Today, modern buildings are replacing these ancient structures. But Meizhou is applying for UNESCO recognition. The Dragon House deserves to stand — not just as architecture, but as a philosophy carved in stone and wood.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 35: Kaiping Grey Carving / 开平灰雕</h3>

<p>On the walls of Kaiping's iconic watchtowers and ancestral halls, you'll find exquisite grey carvings that locals call &quot;the flowers that never wither.&quot; They've survived a century of tropical storms, scorching heat, and humid Lingnan summers — and they still look stunning.</p>

<p>Kaiping Grey Carving dates back to the mid-Qing Dynasty, about 300 years ago. The technique is deceptively simple-looking but extraordinarily labor-intensive. First, lime is soaked into slurry. Dry straw is cut into sections, soaked layer by layer to make &quot;grass gluten ash.&quot; Then comes the crucial step: steeping the mixture for about 20 days before grinding it into paste, and adding glutinous rice flour and brown sugar to create &quot;paper ash&quot; — the surface material.</p>

<p>On the wall, craftsmen use steel nails and copper wire as armature, building up layers of grass ash before finishing with paper ash. The result? A relief sculpture that's both weatherproof and exquisitely detailed.</p>

<p>The subjects are deeply auspicious: monkeys playing with balls, phoenixes facing the sun, the Eight Immortals crossing the sea. But what makes Kaiping grey carving unique is its fusion with Western elements. When overseas Chinese returned from abroad, they brought new materials (cement) and new motifs — Baroque floral patterns, trains, steamships, skyscrapers. The grey carvings became a visual diary of a culture in conversation with the world.</p>

<p>Today this art form is endangered — modern construction materials have pushed traditional techniques aside. But as a living record of Kaiping's unique history as the &quot;hometown of overseas Chinese,&quot; these grey flowers deserve to bloom forever.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 36: Chaozhou Paifang (Archways) / 潮州牌坊</h3>

<p>Chaozhou's Paifang Street is unlike anywhere else in China. While most cities have scattered memorial archways, Chaozhou concentrated 22 of them on a single street — the largest ancient archway street in the country. It's earned the nickname &quot;First Paifang Street in the World.&quot;</p>

<p>These aren't just decorative arches. From the Zhou Dynasty onward, Chinese archways (paifang or pailou) served as official monuments of recognition. In Chaozhou, they specifically honored two types of people: those who excelled in the imperial examinations, and those who rendered outstanding service to the state.</p>

<p>The construction peaked during the Ming and Qing dynasties, particularly under Emperors Jiajing and Qianlong. On average, a new archway appeared every three years. That tells you something about Chaozhou at the time: it was teeming with talent and ambition.</p>

<p>Building a paifang wasn't easy. The honoree had to be at minimum a Jinshi (the highest imperial examination degree). Local officials had to petition the emperor himself for approval. And the honored family paid for most of the construction — the government contributed only 30 taels of silver. So each archway represented both exceptional achievement and substantial wealth.</p>

<p>The names tell the story: &quot;Four Jinshi Archway,&quot; &quot;Five Sages,&quot; &quot;Six Sages,&quot; &quot;Seven Talents&quot; — each commemorating a cohort of remarkable individuals who brought glory to their hometown. The arches themselves are artworks, with calligraphy and carvings by famous masters on both sides.</p>

<p>Today, Paifang Street has been revived as a commercial and cultural hub. But walk down it, look up at those 22 arches, and you're walking through a history book carved in stone — monument after monument to human excellence.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 37: Liuyi Pavilion, Dongguan / 东莞六一一亭</h3>

<p>In the quiet water town of Daojiao, Dongguan, a modest memorial stands as one of the most important records of Japanese wartime atrocities in Lingnan.</p>

<p>The Liuyi Pavilion — officially named the National Martyr Tomb — marks a tragedy that unfolded on the 11th day of the intercalary sixth lunar month in 1941. Japanese soldiers, conducting a retaliatory massacre, trapped 61 villagers inside a Lu family residence and released poisonous smoke. All 61 died.</p>

<p>After the war, the villagers transformed the site into a memorial. The structure is deceptively simple — 8.6 meters wide, 20 meters long, divided into a front gate, courtyard, main pavilion, and rear tomb. But inside the walls are 19 stone inscriptions from the most prominent figures of Republican-era Guangdong — Kuomintang generals and officials.</p>

<p>General Jiang Guangnai, the famous抗日 commander, personally wrote the plaque &quot;Liuyi Pavilion&quot; that hangs above the entrance. General Li Fulin inscribed the couplets: &quot;Hero's blood is always new and the soul is immortal; the river is like a picture of the return of the fierce soul.&quot; Other inscriptions read &quot;National Righteousness,&quot; &quot;Spirit of the Nation,&quot; &quot;Rather Die Than Surrender,&quot; &quot;Sacrifice to Achieve Justice.&quot;</p>

<p>Dongguan magistrate He Efang wrote the main stele, praising common people whose &quot;loyalty and dignity are worth our admiring and following.&quot;</p>

<p>Unlike grand war memorials, Liuyi Pavilion sits quietly among residential buildings, a modest gray structure on a busy street. But its message is anything but modest. Every Qingming Festival, local schoolchildren pay respects. The memorial doesn't seek vengeance — it asks simply that we never forget what ordinary people suffered, and that we treasure peace.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 38: Feng Ru's Former Residence / 冯如故居纪念馆</h3>

<p>Long before China built its own space station, there was Feng Ru — a young man from a tiny village in Enping who became the father of Chinese aviation.</p>

<p>Born in 1884, Feng Ru (also known as Feng Jiuru) showed genius early, building clever toys that amazed his neighbors. At 10, his father died. He followed relatives to San Francisco and became a mechanical apprentice.</p>

<p>By 1906, with financial backing from overseas Chinese in America, he founded the &quot;Guangdong Manufacturing Machinery Factory&quot; and began building aircraft. On September 21, 1909 — just six years after the Wright Brothers — Feng Ru successfully flew his first manned powered aircraft. He kept improving the design through seven iterations.</p>

<p>In January 1911, Feng Ru performed a public flight in Oakland. In the audience was Dr. Sun Yat-sen, who declared: &quot;There are many talents in our country!&quot; Americans offered Feng Ru huge sums to stay. He refused. Instead, he dismantled his planes, packed everything onto ships, and returned to China.</p>

<p>After the 1911 Revolution, he was appointed commander of the Guangdong Revolutionary Army's air corps. But tragedy struck on August 25, 1912. During a flight demonstration in Guangzhou, his plane malfunctioned. Feng Ru died in the crash. He was 28. His body rests today in Guangzhou's Huanghuagang — the same cemetery as the 72 Martyrs.</p>

<p>His childhood home in Liantang Village, Enping, is preserved as a museum — a modest 52-square-meter Qing-era brick house with his portrait, plane models, and even his wedding supplies intact. A six-story memorial building nearby displays his photos, letters, and press coverage from around the world.</p>

<p>From a village boy to the first Chinese man to fly — Feng Ru's story soars.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Part 4: Food & Cuisine</h2>

<h3>Story 39: Cudweed Cake / Shuikeguo (鼠壳粿)</h3>

<p>There's a smell that says &quot;New Year is coming&quot; in every Chaoshan kitchen — and it's the earthy, sweet aroma of Shuikeguo, or Cudweed Cake, steaming on banana leaves.</p>

<p>Legend says this humble snack was born out of desperation during the Southern Song Dynasty. As Yuan soldiers invaded, displaced villagers foraged for wild plants and discovered gnaphalium — a fragrant, harmless weed that could fill their empty stomachs. They ground it with rice into a paste, steamed it, and called it &quot;Shuikeguo.&quot; Later they realized the weed could be dried and stored, so the cakes could be made for any festival.</p>

<p>Over centuries, the recipe evolved. The rice crust was swapped for soft glutinous rice, giving it that signature elasticity. The filling graduated from simple sweet potato and brown sugar to luxurious mung bean paste, red bean paste, or crushed peanuts with sugar. The result? A dark green, glossy cake with a soft, chewy texture that practically dissolves on your tongue.</p>

<p>The making is a ritual: wash and boil the gnaphalium, soak it for days to remove bitterness, pound it into cotton-like fluff, knead it into sticky rice dough, wrap in sweet filling, press into carved wooden molds lined with banana leaves, and steam until fragrant. Hot from the steamer, it's impossibly soft — almost melting in your mouth. Cold leftovers? Pan-fry until crispy on both sides — a completely different but equally addictive experience.</p>

<p>Li Shizhen's Compendium of Materia Medica even mentions gnaphalium cakes from the Ming Dynasty, proving this tradition is over 400 years old. To Chaoshan people, Shuikeguo isn't just food. It's the taste of homecoming. When mother starts making these cakes, you know the new year is near — and that faraway children will soon be back around the table.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 40: Beef Tendon Cake / Niujingao (牛筋糕)</h3>

<p>Look at a piece of Niujingao, and you'd swear it's real beef tendon — amber-yellow, translucent, impossibly chewy. One bite and you realize: this is pure glutinous rice magic, and there's not a speck of meat inside.</p>

<p>Beef Tendon Cakes come from Longchuan, Heyuan City, a county nestled deep in Guangdong's Hakka heartland. For over 300 years, since the Qing Dynasty, locals have been perfecting this unique confection. The name comes from its appearance — the cakes are so yellow and tough that they look exactly like beef tendon. But it's the texture, not the ingredient, that gives this snack its legendary name.</p>

<p>The story goes that a clever vendor once tricked curious passersby into buying what he called &quot;Beef Tendon Cakes.&quot; People gathered around, puzzled and intrigued. But when they tasted it — the unique chewiness, the subtle sweetness releasing slowly as you bite — they were hooked immediately. Word spread from street to street, and the name stuck forever.</p>

<p>Making Niujingao is an art of patience. Premium glutinous rice flour is kneaded into a stiff dough, steamed, and then pounded repeatedly until it develops that signature springy, dense texture. The result is a cake that's hard but not greasy, soft but not mushy — striking the perfect balance that local chefs have guarded as a family secret for generations. No additives, just pure simplicity.</p>

<p>In 1985, it won the prestigious Guangdong Cake Quality Award. Today, it ships worldwide from this small Hakka town, each piece topped with white sesame seeds, still cut with scissors the old-fashioned way. Grab a slice, take your time, and chew slowly — the sweetness creeps up on you, just like the centuries-old story behind it.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 41: Golden Crispy Glutinous Cakes / Jinsu Youci (金酥油糍)</h3>

<p>Walk into any Guangxi home during the New Year, and you'll find a family gathered around a bubbling wok of oil, laughter mixing with the sizzle. They're making Jinsu Youci — Golden Crispy Glutinous Cakes, a festive tradition that brings everyone together.</p>

<p>Don't confuse these with ordinary glutinous rice cakes. Jinsu Youci is in a league of its own. Picture this: a palm-sized, olive-shaped dough made from glutinous rice flour and melted brown sugar, stuffed with a mixture of crushed peanuts, sesame seeds, and white sugar. Then each one is gently lowered into nearly-boiling peanut oil — but never too many at once, just enough to cover the surface.</p>

<p>Watch closely. The cakes start pale yellow, bobbing at the bottom. As they heat, they rise to the surface — slowly swelling into golden, puffed-up spheres. The cook flips them constantly with chopsticks for even browning. Five to six minutes later, they emerge: golden, round, and gleaming. The moment you bite, warm melted sugar mingles with crunchy peanut and sesame pieces, flooding your mouth with sweetness. The outside shatters, the inside melts — and the aroma fills the entire room.</p>

<p>The texture is everything — shatteringly crisp on the outside, tender and yielding within, never greasy. Each olive-shaped cake is perfectly sized for one satisfying serving. And somehow, one is never enough — you'll reach for another before you've finished chewing.</p>

<p>But in Guangxi homes, Jinsu Youci is more than a snack. It's an occasion. Relatives gather around the table, chatting as they knead and wrap the dough. It's said that eating these cakes isn't really about the food itself. Making them together, laughing together, and sharing the warmth of family — that's the purest joy there is.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 42: Limulus Kueh / Houguo (鲎粿)</h3>

<p>In the ancient county of Miancheng, Chaoyang, there's a snack that locals have guarded jealously for over 300 years. It's called Houguo — Limulus Kueh — and it's the kind of food that makes you plan your next trip back before you've even swallowed.</p>

<p>The name comes from &quot;limulus&quot; (horseshoe crab), an ancient sea creature. Local legend says a daughter-in-law created this dish for her toothless mother-in-law who couldn't chew. Others say it was born from scarcity, when people used horseshoe crab blood to bind the rice paste. Whatever the origin story, the result is pure genius.</p>

<p>Making Houguo is a two-act performance. First, rice paste is blended with tapioca flour, poured into special molds, and loaded with treasures: minced pork, whole shrimp, quail eggs, mushrooms, dried squid if you're fancy. Steam until set.</p>

<p>Act two: the fried transformation. Those steamed kueh go into hot oil until their surfaces turn golden and shatteringly crisp. Scooped out, drained, scissors-snipped open, then drizzled with soy sauce and chili paste. Every bite delivers a cascade of textures — the brittle shell giving way to soft, savory filling, the briny shrimp popping against earthy mushrooms, the chili cutting through the richness.</p>

<p>This isn't a once-a-year treat. You can find Houguo on Miancheng's streets any season — sold from humble stalls, served on scratched white plates, eaten with toothpicks while standing. The golden color alone makes you hungry. But it's that first crunch, followed by the warm, savory explosion inside, that keeps you coming back.</p>

<p>If you ever visit Chaoyang, let a local take you to the old quarter. First, the Man Kwong Tower for ancient solemnity. Then, a tiny shop for the best Houguo of your life.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 43: Pig-and-Sweet-Rice-Cake Festival (摆猪羊和甜粿年)</h3>

<p>In Xinheng Town, Jiedong County, there's a village called Shuolian where time moves differently. Their most important festival rotates among ten natural villages — meaning each village gets to host only once a decade. And when it's your turn, you go ALL OUT.</p>

<p>Called &quot;Laying Pigs and Goats,&quot; this tradition dates back over 500 years to the Ming Dynasty. Wealthy merchant families began raising fat pigs and goats, slaughtering them, and laying them on altar tables to worship Zhao Gongming and Guan Gong — the gods of wealth. After the ceremony, the meat was distributed to neighbors and relatives as a gesture of goodwill and harmony.</p>

<p>Over centuries, the tradition grew. Today, 52 family names across 20 natural villages participate, but only 10 major villages rotate the honor annually. In addition to the pig-and-goat sacrifice, they have &quot;Sweet Glutinous Rice Year&quot; and &quot;Goose Year&quot; — each village draws different duties. Sweet rice cakes — a Chaoshan dessert specialty — are offered on massive platters alongside whole roasted geese.</p>

<p>The preparation is breathtaking. The host village starts planning a full year ahead. Before Spring Festival, every alley is strung with colorful lanterns. Every doorway sports fresh red couplets. On the big day, the village explodes with energy — gongs and drums shake the air, Chao opera performances echo through the streets, and the smell of roasted meat and sweet rice fills every corner.</p>

<p>The ceremony isn't just about worship. It's the village showing itself to the world — demonstrating unity, prosperity, and the joy of community. In 2009, this magnificent tradition was recognized as a Guangdong Provincial Intangible Cultural Heritage.</p>

<p>For locals, these few days are the most anticipated of the year. It's not just a festival — it's identity, memory, and the hope that the next decade brings even more abundance.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 44: Bamboo-Pressed Noodles / Zhusheng Mian (竹升面)</h3>

<p>Walk down any street in Guangzhou, and you'll see them: tiny noodle shops with steam billowing from giant cauldrons. Inside, a chef is doing something peculiar — straddling a thick bamboo pole, bouncing his entire body weight to press a mound of dough.</p>

<p>This is Zhusheng Mian — Bamboo-Pressed Noodles. The name comes from the method: the master uses a long bamboo pole (zhusheng) as a lever, pressing and folding the dough rhythmically. It's punishing work. But the result is noodle perfection: thin, slightly yellow, impossibly springy with a unique chew that machine-made noodles can never replicate.</p>

<p>The secret ingredient is alkaline water, which gives the noodles their distinctive yellow hue and removes excess acidity, creating that clean taste Cantonese chefs call &quot;freshness.&quot; When cooked, the noodles are called &quot;silver-thread fine noodles&quot; — delicate, silky, with the perfect al dente bite.</p>

<p>Order a bowl at any neighborhood shop. Watch through the glass as the chef tosses fresh noodles into a small wire basket with wonton or fish balls, hangs it on the edge of the boiling cauldron. The rich pork bone broth has been simmering for hours. When it's ready, the noodles are poured into a deep bowl, topped with broth, sprinkled with chopped scallion and chives, garnished with bright green lettuce.</p>

<p>Pick up your chopsticks. Lift a strand. It trembles slightly, gleaming with broth. Slurp. The texture is astonishing — springy, smooth, with a subtle sweetness that builds with every bite. The broth is clean but deeply savory. Add a dash of red vinegar for that extra Cantonese touch.</p>

<p>From day to night, from grandpas to schoolkids, Guangzhou fills these shops. Zhusheng Mian isn't fancy — it's humble, honest, and utterly perfect.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 45: Puning Emblica / Puning Yougan (普宁油柑)</h3>

<p>Don't judge a fruit by its first bite. Especially not Puning Emblica.</p>

<p>Round, smooth, pale yellow when perfectly ripe — Puning Emblica looks innocent enough. But pop one in your mouth and brace yourself. The first chew releases an explosion of bitter, astringent juice that makes first-timers instinctively spit it out. But if you persist — if you bite again, let the juice settle — something magical happens.</p>

<p>A wave of cool,清爽 sweetness rises from your tongue, spreading through your entire mouth. The bitterness transforms into a pleasant, lingering aftertaste that the Chinese call &quot;hui gan&quot; — returning sweetness. This is the emblica's great trick: making you work for the reward.</p>

<p>This humble fruit has been prized in Chaoshan for centuries. The Chinese saying goes: &quot;The more you eat emblica, the younger you become.&quot; In Puning, almost every family with land has a few emblica trees. When harvest season comes, branches bend under heavy clusters of fruit. Vendors line the streets with baskets overflowing with emblica, and locals line up to buy them by the kilo.</p>

<p>People chew emblica after meals, savoring the juice more than the flesh itself. Spit out the small pit, drink a cup of warm water, and your mouth fills with an astonishing sweetness that seems to come from nowhere.</p>

<p>But emblica isn't just tasty — it's medicine. Li Shizhen's Compendium of Materia Medica recorded its &quot;body-lightening, life-prolonging&quot; effects over 400 years ago. Modern science confirms: emblica fights gastric cancer, treats hepatitis, lowers blood pressure and blood sugar, and has anti-aging properties. The FAO lists it among the &quot;World's Three Major Miscellaneous Fruits.&quot;</p>

<p>Bitter becomes sweet. Old becomes young. That's the magic of Puning Emblica.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 46: Jiexi Cuisine (揭西美食)</h3>

<p>Ask any Jiexi native what they miss most when away from home, and they'll rattle off a list that makes your mouth water. This small Hakka county in Guangdong is a treasure chest of culinary traditions that most of the world has never heard of.</p>

<p>Start with Leicha — pounded tea, the crown jewel of Jiexi cuisine. Born in Hepo Town, this isn't your ordinary cup of tea. Tea leaves, roasted peanuts, and sesame seeds go into a special grinding bowl (the lei bowl) and are pounded into a fragrant paste. Add a pinch of salt, pour in boiling water, and the aroma hits you instantly. But here's the twist: Leicha is served over rice, mixed into porridge, topped with fried shrimp, peanuts, leeks, and preserved vegetables. It's a meal, a drink, and a ritual all in one.</p>

<p>Then there's Shuiban — translucent rice sheets steamed paper-thin, wrapped around minced pork, green beans, bamboo shoots, and pickles. It's delicate, refreshing, and you can eat plate after plate without feeling heavy.</p>

<p>Hongtaoban (peach-shaped rice cakes) are pressed into carved wooden molds with beautiful floral patterns, steamed, and sometimes fried until the outside crackles. Wanban rice cakes bloom like flowers as they steam — the cracked top is considered lucky, a smile from the cake foretelling a happy year ahead.</p>

<p>Jiexi fried tofu deserves its own paragraph. Thin-skinned, impossibly crispy outside, snowy white and silky inside — locals call it &quot;gold outside, silver inside.&quot; In winter, biting into a piece of freshly fried tofu is a sensory event: the crunch, the steam, the dribble of chili soy sauce, the sweat beading on your forehead.</p>

<p>And don't forget preserved radish — Jiexi Caipu — aged, sun-dried, and packed into bottles by grandmothers for their children working in faraway cities. It's love, preserved.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 47: Gaoming Laifen / Rice Noodles (高明濑粉)</h3>

<p>In Gaoming, Foshan, the question isn't whether you'll eat Laifen today — it's which toppings you'll choose.</p>

<p>These handmade rice noodles are the soul of this small district. The name comes from the making process: rice dough is pushed (&quot;lai&quot; in local dialect) through wooden molds into boiling water, where it sets into long, smooth, springy strands. The texture depends entirely on the kneader's strength — too soft and the noodles fall apart; too hard and they're tough. It takes generations of experience to get it right.</p>

<p>The soup base is the second pillar: pork bones simmered for hours until the broth turns milky white and impossibly rich. Then come the toppings — each with symbolic meaning. Scallion for vitality. Ginger for empathy. Garlic for good fortune. Crushed peanuts for brilliant writing. Egg ribbons for a wonderful present life. It's a bowl of well-wishes in noodle form.</p>

<p>The history stretches back over 500 years. Legend says the making technique was passed from the ancient Yao people to the Han Chinese of Gaoming, who were expert rice farmers. In the old days, when Gaoming was poor, families would save the late-season rice after harvest, grind it into flour, and seal it in jars — only to be opened for festivals. That's how precious Laifen was.</p>

<p>Since 2006, October 13th has been the official &quot;Gaoming Laifen Festival&quot; — now in its 13th consecutive year. Master artisans demonstrate the traditional hand-making technique while visitors line up for tasting bowls. The festival has become a symbol of Gaoming's pride.</p>

<p>There's even a folk tale: a villager named Li Shiwan, returning from martial arts training, was shunned by neighbors. On New Year's Eve, every stove in the village mysteriously disappeared. Only Li could bring them back — teaching the villagers to cherish their hearths and the noodles cooked on them. Ever since, Gaoming families guard their stoves on New Year's Eve, cooking Laifen through the night.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 48: Shanwei Ground Tea / Shanwei Leicha (汕尾擂茶)</h3>

<p>In Shanwei, hospitality has a sound: the rhythmic thump-thump-thump of a wooden mallet against a clay bowl. That's Leicha — ground tea — being made.</p>

<p>The tools are called the &quot;Three Treasures of Ground Tea&quot;: a pottery bowl (&quot;tooth bowl&quot;) with rough grooves carved into its inner walls, a half-meter pestle made from hawthorn or tea oil wood, and a bamboo sieve for straining. Making Leicha is a three-step meditation. &quot;Rough&quot; — crush the tea leaves coarsely. &quot;Smooth&quot; — grind the sesame seeds evenly into paste without forcing them into the grooves. &quot;Tenacity&quot; — as peanuts and other ingredients pile in, the grinding gets harder. You must persevere.</p>

<p>The recipe is deceptively simple. Tea leaves (broken ones are fine), a few mint leaves, go into the bowl. The maker sits, clamps the bowl between knees with a towel, and grinds clockwise until everything becomes a fragrant paste. Sprinkle in salt. Pour in boiling water. Instantly, the room fills with an intoxicating herbal aroma.</p>

<p>But Leicha is just the beginning. Locals pour it over rice to make a hearty porridge, adding fried shrimp, peanuts, scallions, leeks, garlic, and preserved vegetables. In Haifeng County, they make &quot;Vegetable Tea&quot; — adding chopped spinach, celery, lettuce, mushrooms, vermicelli, lean pork, and even squid, simmered in bone or chicken broth, topped with puffed rice. On the 13th day of the first lunar month, every home in Haifeng is lit with lanterns and filled with the fragrance of Vegetable Tea.</p>

<p>The more elaborate &quot;Oil Tea&quot; uses fresh mugwort, mint, perilla, maple buds, coriander, and medicinal herbs — a pure natural health drink that locals swear keeps them young. In Luhe County, Leicha with rice is the daily noon meal. And on the 7th day of the New Year, seven vegetables are ground together for &quot;Seven-Vegetable Tea,&quot; a tradition that celebrates renewal.</p>

<p>In 2011 and 2012, Shanwei Leicha was recognized as a Provincial Intangible Cultural Heritage. Legend has it that the great musician Ma Sicong, on his deathbed, sighed: &quot;What I regret most is that I can no longer go back to Haifeng to drink Leicha.&quot;</p>

<p>And that's the power of this humble tea — it stays with you, wherever you go.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Part 5: Landmarks & Crafts</h2>

<h3>Story 49: Anpu Ancient Town</h3>

<p>Some towns live quietly in history books. Anpu Ancient Town lives loudly in the present.</p>

<p>Founded in the Ming Dynasty, Anpu was once known as &quot;Anpu&quot; (the dark town) before its 1819 renaming signaled a new chapter. By the Qing Dynasty it had earned its place among the &quot;Four Great Ancient Towns of Guangdong&quot; — a bustling trade hub still buzzing today.</p>

<p>What makes Anpu special isn't just age. It's the living traditions.</p>

<p>Take the town-level Zhongshan Park, the very first in China when it opened in 1931. Ravaged during the war, it was painstakingly rebuilt into the lush sanctuary locals now fill with laughter. Then there's Wenbi Tower — a seven-story, 33-meter pagoda rebuilt in 1993 after being wrongly demolished decades earlier. Inside stands the statue of Wenquxingjun, the god of wisdom. Scholars still come to burn incense and pray for academic success.</p>

<p>And the music! Anpu Baixi (&quot;White Opera&quot;) was born here in the Qianlong era — bamboo pipes, wooden puppets, and a distinctly local dialect that evolved into Western Guangdong's unique puppet opera tradition. Then there's Anpu Bayin, an ensemble of 22 musicians playing instruments from handbells to erxian. During Lantern Festival and Dragon Boat Festival, they still parade through town in long gowns and red hats, ancient melodies spilling through modern streets.</p>

<p>This isn't a preserved relic. It's a five-hundred-year heartbeat, still going strong.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 50: Dahao Ancient City</h3>

<p>You could walk the entire perimeter of Dahao Ancient City in ten minutes. And you absolutely should.</p>

<p>China's best-preserved pocket ancient city sits tucked away in Shantou's Haojiang District, a mere 0.014 square kilometers of Qing Dynasty history hiding in plain sight. Built in 1717 under Governor Yang Lin of Guangdong and Guangxi, its purpose was simple: stop the pirates who terrorized local merchants along this strategic stretch of coast.</p>

<p>The walls run 494 meters total — 5 meters high, 1.3 meters thick, with watchtowers at all four corners. Two gates face each other (Dashan and Xihao), connected by a single 100-meter street, the only road in town. Stand at one gate; you can clearly make out the other.</p>

<p>Inside this tiny fortress, Qing administrators packed everything a regional center needed: a naval garrison in the west, a police inspection office in the east, and a salt administration bureau in the north — all arranged in a perfect &quot;品&quot; (pin) character layout.</p>

<p>Today, Dahao hides in plain sight, nestled within a labyrinth of lively alleys. Wander Haipang Sixiang to its end and you'll stumble upon Xihao Gate like a secret. Three centuries of pirates, soldiers, and salt merchants have come and gone. The city — tiny, perfect, resolute — remains.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 51: Meiguan Ancient Road</h3>

<p>Close your eyes and imagine: a narrow stone path cutting through the Meiling Mountains, six feet wide, lined with ancient plum trees. For over two thousand years, this trail was the main artery between northern and southern China.</p>

<p>Meiguan Ancient Road was built during the Qin Dynasty (221-207 BC), making it one of the oldest surviving post roads in the country. It winds from Jiangxi's Zhangshui down to Guangdong's Zhenshui — a living link between the Yangtze and Pearl River systems. At a time when the mountains split China in two, this road held it together.</p>

<p>The road gets its name from the Yue people, led by their chief Meijuan, who settled here during the Warring States era. The plum trees that blanket the mountain — giving Meiling (&quot;Plum Ridge&quot;) its name — have made this one of China's four great plum blossom viewing spots. Every winter, &quot;Yuling Cold Plum&quot; transforms the ancient path into a snow-white dreamscape.</p>

<p>But Meiguan isn't just scenic. It's hallowed ground. Communist leader Chen Yi fought three years of guerrilla warfare here and wrote his immortal &quot;Three Chapters of Meiling&quot; in the face of death. The road witnessed the migration of Han Chinese southward, carrying language, customs, and culture from the Central Plains across the pass.</p>

<p>Today, the railway and highway have replaced its old purpose. But the walk — on neatly laid stones, through plum blossoms, past 2,000 years — that walk is yours to take.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 52: Shuidong Street</h3>

<p>If Shuidong Street could talk, it would tell you stories of emperors, poets, and the resilience of a city.</p>

<p>Built during the Northern Song Dynasty (1078-1085), this 750-meter arcade street in Huizhou was where three cultures met — Cantonese, Chaoshan, and Hakka — colliding and blending into something uniquely Lingnan. By the late Qing and early Republic era, it was the commercial heart of the city: inns, cloth shops, pharmacies, groceries, pawnshops, all tucked under its iconic covered walkways.</p>

<p>The arcades (qilou) themselves are works of art. Traditional Chinese &quot;gold character&quot; tile roofs, exquisite wood carvings on windows and doors, and two systems of pipes on the walls — thick ones for smoke, thin ones for drainage. The structure creates a covered corridor on each side, so shoppers never get wet in the rain. Upstairs, families lived; downstairs, commerce hummed. From upper-floor windows, you could watch boats glide along the Dongjiang River while crowds swirled below — a modern &quot;Riverside Scene at Qingming Festival.&quot;</p>

<p>Then came war. During the Anti-Japanese War, Japanese forces burned over half the street and 200 shops to ashes. Rebuilt but never quite the same, it waited.</p>

<p>Today, Shuidong Street has risen again. Three parallel lanes — one a pedestrian walkway — blend restored Song-era architecture with modern touches. At the eastern end, Hejiang Attic, one of Guangdong's six famous towers, stands guard. Su Dongpo, China's beloved poet, lived here for over a year and wrote: &quot;I'd rather live in Lingnan forever.&quot;</p>

<p>Stand on the dock at night, feeling the river breeze, watching the city lights flicker across the water. You'll understand exactly what he meant.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 53: The Ancient Kingdom of Fulou</h3>

<p>For centuries, history books dismissed the Lingnan region as a &quot;barbarian land&quot; — a place of miasma and backwardness where Central Plains emperors exiled their unwanted officials. Then Boluo County, Huizhou, started digging.</p>

<p>In 1985, seven perfectly preserved bronze artifacts and chime bells from the Spring and Autumn Period were unearthed. More followed in 1996 and 2000: pottery, porcelain, jade, crystal — a treasure trove of advanced craftsmanship that shattered every old assumption.</p>

<p>The writings of Lu Buwei (the Qin dynasty chancellor) had mentioned a place called &quot;Fulou&quot; in the south, alongside other mysterious kingdoms. Most scholars assumed it was folklore. The artifacts proved otherwise.</p>

<p>The Kingdom of Fulou was real. And it was sophisticated.</p>

<p>Its pottery industry was extraordinary — a network of dragon kilns spanning the Dongjiang River basin, leading experts to call Huizhou one of the birthplaces of southern Chinese ceramics. Its bronze work rivaled the Central Plains, yet retained unique local designs and patterns. The tombs unearthed at the Fulou site are the richest, most exquisite Bronze Age burials ever found in Guangdong, showcasing a civilization that was connected to the north while fiercely maintaining its own identity.</p>

<p>Founded by the Luo clan during the Warring States period, Fulou vanished around 337 BC. Why it rose and fell, we may never fully know. But its spirit — open, humble, adaptive yet unyielding — lives on in Lingnan culture today.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 54: Mei'an Temple</h3>

<p>In the hills of Zhaoqing, where the North Ridge meets the West River, a thousand-year-old temple guards the memory of one of Buddhism's greatest teachers.</p>

<p>Mei'an Temple — &quot;Plum Blossom Nunnery&quot; — was built in 996 AD on the very spot where Hui Neng, the legendary Sixth Patriarch of Chinese Chan (Zen) Buddhism, planted plum trees. The story goes like this:</p>

<p>Hui Neng had a deep love for plum blossoms. Wherever he traveled, he planted them as markers. Passing through Duanzhou (now Zhaoqing), he climbed a hill and knew at once — this was a sacred spot. He planted a plum tree, then thrust his tin staff into the dry ground. When he pulled it out, pure spring water burst from the earth. The well filled instantly. A monk tasted it: sweet as mellow wine.</p>

<p>Hui Neng told the gathered monks, &quot;This soil is perfect for plum blossoms.&quot; The next day, they planted plum trees around the hill.</p>

<p>The temple that rose here centuries later preserves these wonders. The Daxiong (Great Buddha) Hall still stands in its original Song Dynasty brick-and-wood structure. The Liuzu Well — Hui Neng's well — still produces clear, sweet water. Every year when plum blossoms blanket the grounds, pilgrims and tourists follow the fragrance up the hill, tracing the footsteps of a master who believed that enlightenment could bloom from a single planted branch.</p>

<p>A national protected heritage site since 1996, Mei'an isn't just a temple. It's a place where faith took root and flowered.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 55: Chishi Guanyin Temple</h3>

<p>High in the Duruan Town hills of Jiangmen, a mountain hides a miracle.</p>

<p>Chishi Mountain — the &quot;Shouting-Stone Mountain&quot; — takes its name from a legend so vivid that pilgrims have been coming here for centuries. At its heart is Chishi Guanyin Temple, where stone sheep dot the hillsides and a Taoist fairy tale comes to life.</p>

<p>The story begins with Huang Chuping, a poor shepherd boy from what is now Jinhua, Zhejiang. At eight years old, he began herding sheep. At fifteen, he met an immortal who led him into a cave to study the mysteries of Taoism. For forty years, Huang ate no worldly food, saw no worldly people, and devoted himself entirely to enlightenment.</p>

<p>His brother, Huang Chuqi, searched for him all those decades. Finally, a fortune-telling Taoist pointed him toward Jinhua Mountain. There, in a cave, he found his brother — exactly as he had looked forty years before.</p>

<p>&quot;Where are the sheep?&quot; Chuqi asked.</p>

<p>&quot;In the east,&quot; Chuping replied. But when they reached the mountain slope, there was nothing but white boulders.</p>

<p>Chuping laughed. &quot;The sheep are here. You simply can't see them.&quot; He shouted: &quot;CHI! CHI! ARISE!&quot; And the white stones rose, transforming into a flock of living sheep.</p>

<p>Dumbfounded, Chuqi believed. He too began to practice. In time, both brothers became immortals. Huang Chuping is worshipped today as Wong Tai Sin, the Great Immortal Huang, and his footprints — and those of his fairy boy companion — are said to remain on Chishi Mountain to this day.</p>

<p>Step onto the mountain path, surrounded by stone sheep and the scent of incense. You'll understand why legends survive.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 56: Nan'ao Song Well</h3>

<p>When a Song emperor is fleeing for his life, even water becomes a symbol of hope.</p>

<p>In the late Southern Song Dynasty (13th century), Mongol forces were sweeping south. The imperial court fled from Lin'an (modern Hangzhou), proclaimed a new emperor in Fuzhou, then sailed south by sea. Escorted by General Zhang Shijie and Minister Lu Xiufu, they landed on Nan'ao Island in what is now Shantou, Guangdong.</p>

<p>Fresh water was scarce. So they dug three wells: Dragon Well for the emperor, Tiger Well for his ministers, Horse Well for soldiers and attendants.</p>

<p>Seven centuries later, only one of those wells has ever been found. And it's a miracle.</p>

<p>The surviving Horse Well sits just 10 meters from the surging South China Sea. Tides and sand have buried it countless times over the centuries, only for storms or chance to reveal it again. And here's the mystery that defies explanation: when the tide recedes and the well reappears, the water inside is perfectly fresh — pure, sweet, clear, and drinkable.</p>

<p>Locals call it &quot;Magic Songjing.&quot; Scientists have theories — a hidden freshwater spring, a geological anomaly, an underground filtration system. But standing beside that ancient well, smelling the salt sea spray on one side and tasting sweet well water drawn from the other, you feel something deeper.</p>

<p>Three wells, one emperor, one fleeing dynasty, one mystery still unsolved. The Dragon Well and Tiger Well have never been found. They're out there somewhere, buried beneath sand and tide, waiting.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 57: History of Siulam (Lan Clan)</h3>

<p>Xiaolan, now a town in Zhongshan, was once a cluster of islands at the Pearl River mouth. Its name comes from hills shaped like olives. Though small, Xiaolan carries a rich history woven from centuries of migration.</p>

<p>The history of Xiaolan is the history of the Pearl River Delta. People from the Song, Yuan, Ming, and Qing dynasties settled here, bringing traditions like lion dance, salt water songs, and puppet shows — now Xiaolan intangible cultural heritage.</p>

<p>Powerful clans emerged — especially the He, Li, and Mai families. He Wuzhu became a grand secretary of the Ming Dynasty; Li Sunchen served as Minister of Rites. Their ancestral halls became local landmarks.</p>

<p>But Xiaolan is most famous for one thing: chrysanthemums. Known as &quot;Chrysanthemum City,&quot; its flower culture runs deep. The first poetry society of Xiangshan County was born here. Literati would gather when chrysanthemums bloomed, drinking wine and writing poems — calling themselves the &quot;Chrysanthemum Society.&quot; In the Qianlong period, it became the &quot;Jiaxu Chrysanthemum Conference.&quot;</p>

<p>Today, the exhibitions run on a cycle: small show every year, medium every three, larger every ten, and grand every sixty years. Legend tells of imperial concubines fleeing to Xiaolan and planting chrysanthemums.</p>

<p>And then there's the food. Chrysanthemum petals with sugar, chrysanthemum wine, chrysanthemum sprinkled on rice pudding. The most famous: chrysanthemum fish balls — so popular that three shops from Xiaolan became renowned across Guangdong, Hong Kong, and Macau.</p>

<p>The philosopher Zhuangzi wrote: &quot;Water that is not deep enough cannot carry a large boat.&quot; Xiaolan's deep cultural waters carry stories still waiting to be discovered.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 58: Chaozhou Embroidery</h3>

<p>Chaozhou Embroidery, together with Guangzhou Embroidery, forms Guangdong Embroidery — one of China's Four Famous Embroideries alongside Suzhou, Hunan, and Sichuan.</p>

<p>Its history begins in the Tang Dynasty, with a distinctive style emerging during the Ming and Qing. By the Qianlong period (1736-1795), embroidery shops lined the streets of Chaozhou. Twenty shops clustered around Tianditan, Bushu Street, and Kaiyuan Temple alone. Exports flowed to Singapore, Thailand, and Malaysia, where Chaozhou embroidery decorated halls and Buddhist temples in Southeast Asia.</p>

<p>The 19th-century British artist Stephen Wootton Bushell wrote in his book Chinese Art: &quot;The Chinese are good at embroidering flowers and birds, and the Cantonese are especially good at this technique.&quot; Chaozhou embroidery had already reached Europe by then.</p>

<p>The art reached its peak in the 1960s and 70s. One hundred thousand embroiderers worked across towns and villages, producing over 100 million yuan in value. Children learned from their parents, mastering difficult techniques. Flat embroidery and bead embroidery flourished — and Chaozhou became the world's largest producer of beaded evening gowns and wedding dresses.</p>

<p>There are four main types: woolen embroidery (for wall hangings), silk thread embroidery, gold and silver thread embroidery (with unique &quot;two-needle scale&quot; technique), and gold velvet embroidery.</p>

<p>The embroidery is known for its rigorous design, rich colors, and three-dimensional effect. The raised embroidery technique creates a relief effect unique among Chinese embroideries. Dragons, phoenixes, flowers, and figures burst from the fabric in vivid compositions.</p>

<p>Since the 1950s, Chaozhou embroidery has been a diplomatic gift from Chinese leaders to foreign heads of state. In 2006, it was listed among China's first batch of national intangible cultural heritage.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 59: Zhongxin Festive Lanterns</h3>

<p>Heyuan City, one of China's purest Hakka regions, is home to a unique tradition: the Zhongxin Festive Lantern.</p>

<p>In Hakka dialect, &quot;lantern&quot; sounds like &quot;boy.&quot; So when a baby boy is born, the family holds a ceremony called &quot;Shangdeng&quot; — hanging a lantern in the ancestral hall. The ritual runs from the 11th to 15th day of the first lunar month. The family makes or buys an elegant lantern, lights it, and raises it to one-third the height of the hall — symbolizing the addition of a son.</p>

<p>Zhongxin Town in Lianping County has observed this custom for over 600 years. The ceremony follows six steps: dropping the lantern cord, selecting the lantern, welcoming it, hanging it, lighting it, and ending. Along the way: Hakka drums and gongs, dragon and lion dances, ancestor worship, and festive wine.</p>

<p>The lanterns themselves are masterpieces. Made of woven bamboo, they come in four, six, eight, or twelve corners. Large ones are 12 meters in diameter; small ones measure 40 centimeters. A lantern has three parts: cover, body, and skirt. The skirt is the most distinctive — decorated with colorful light belts in various floral patterns. Inside, rotating images create a dynamic &quot;walking lantern&quot; effect.</p>

<p>The craft involves weaving, printing, cutting, engraving, mounting, and decorating. Zhongxin Town alone has over 10 lantern production sites. Shangliao Community in Shangtang Village is so famous it's called &quot;Lantern Village&quot; — every single villager is a lantern-making inheritor. In 2017, it was officially designated as the Zhongxin Festive Lantern Production Base.</p>

<p>The government holds a lantern festival every two or three years, bringing this 600-year-old tradition out of the ancestral halls and into the world.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 60: Rongchang Folding Fans</h3>

<p>Rongchang Folding Fan, also called &quot;Folding Bone Fan,&quot; is one of the three most famous fans in China — alongside Suzhou Silk Fan and Hangzhou Calligraphy and Painting Fan. In November 2019, it was formally listed as a national intangible cultural heritage.</p>

<p>China has long been known as the kingdom of fan-making. Rongchang fans have been crafted since 1551, the Jiajing period of the Ming Dynasty. By the late Ming, they were already tributes to the imperial court.</p>

<p>The fan looks simple, but making it is anything but. The process involves 16 stages and 145 individual operations. Each stage has its own guild: Danqing Society handles writing and painting, Xunfeng Society pastes the white pages, Qingshan Society cuts the bamboo ribs, Sanxian Society does lacquering and carving — and many more.</p>

<p>The fan is light and dexterous, with an outer-round, inner-square shape and smooth, flowing lines. Today there are 345 varieties using palm, bamboo, sandalwood, silk, feather, leather, and more.</p>

<p>According to the Records of Rongchang County, folding fans were called &quot;head-folding fans&quot; in ancient times and entered China as Japanese tribute to Emperor Taizong of the Tang Dynasty. They spread among officials and commoners during the Song Dynasty and truly became popular in the Ming.</p>

<p>Today, Rongchang Folding Fans serve many purposes: advertising, gifts, souvenirs, stage performances, interior decoration, and art collecting. In 2002, 440 carved calligraphy fans were presented as gifts to foreign guests at the AAPP conference in Chongqing.</p>

<p>They are exported to India, Thailand, Myanmar, Japan, and beyond. And while they may not cool as well as electric fans, their portability and cultural value are impossible to replace.</p>

<hr />

<h3>Story 61: The Three Treasures of Huizhou Culture</h3>

<p>Huizhou, known as &quot;Half City Lake, Half City Mountain,&quot; has been the political and cultural center of the Dongjiang River basin since the Sui and Tang Dynasties. It is one of the most important Hakka settlements in the world — and home to three cultural treasures.</p>

<p>First: the fishing wedding of Daya Bay. Two boats, decorated with the character for &quot;happiness&quot; and colorful flags, sail through the sea. Twenty women lead, wearing red ribbons, gold hairpins, bare feet, holding colored poles. Firecrackers, gongs, and drums sound. Lions (men in costumes) dance on the boat roofs. Modern life has changed the fishing villages, but this tradition survives.</p>

<p>Second: Hakka Han Opera — one of Guangdong's three major opera forms alongside Cantonese and Chaozhou opera. The Huiyang Han Opera Troupe attracted talents from across the region and performed classics like &quot;Hai Rui Dismissed from Office.&quot; The premiere of &quot;Qin Xianglian&quot; at Huizhou People's Hall caused a citywide sensation — every show was packed. Though fewer people sing Han opera today, performances still appear at gatherings and before New Year's Eve.</p>

<p>Third: the &quot;Dongjiang Famous Dishes&quot; — three Hakka culinary masterpieces. Dongjiang salt-baked chicken, stuffed tofu, and the legendary Mei Cai Kou Rou (steamed pork belly with preserved vegetable). The latter is famous worldwide. Su Dongpo, exiled to Huizhou, sent two local chefs to Hangzhou's West Lake to learn cooking techniques. They returned, and Su asked them to adapt Hangzhou's &quot;Dongpo Braised Pork&quot; using local preserved vegetables. The result: Mei Cai Kou Rou — fatty but not greasy, tender and unforgettable.</p>

<p>Huizhou's culture is defined by inclusiveness. Mei Cai Kou Rou adapts an old recipe. The fishing wedding preserves tradition alongside modernity. Han opera welcomes talents from everywhere. This unique blend makes Huizhou one of Lingnan's most fascinating cities.</p>

